**Yesâ€”changing the variables can improve a strategyâ€™s results. But it can also produce â€œextreme profitâ€ on historical data that disappears in live trading.** The task is to find settings that continue working on unseen data, after realistic costs.

For our SMC model, these are the main adjustable variables. **The example values below are research choices, not proven profitable settings.**

| Variable | What it controls | Example values or choices |
|---|---|---|
| **Context timeframe** | Which chart defines the broader direction | M15, H1, H4 |
| **Entry timeframe** | Which chart supplies the trigger | M1, M3, M5, M15 |
| **Swing sensitivity** | How many candles on each side confirm a high/low | 2, 3, or 5 per side |
| **Sweep distance** | How far price must cross a known level | A fixed number of points or a fraction of volatility |
| **Reclaim rule** | What confirms price returned inside the level | Same-candle close or a later close |
| **Structure-break rule** | What qualifies as BOS/CHoCH | Wick crossing versus completed candle close |
| **Displacement threshold** | How strong the confirmation candle must be | Body â‰¥ 0.6, 0.8, or 1.0 Ã— ATR |
| **Setup expiry** | How long confirmation remains eligible | 3, 6, or 12 candles after the sweep |
| **Entry method** | When to enter after confirmation | Next available quote, order-block retest, or FVG retest |
| **Zone definition** | How an order block or FVG is constructed | Full candle range, candle body, minimum gap size |
| **Stop buffer** | Extra distance beyond the invalidation level | 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3 Ã— ATR |
| **Target rule** | Where to take profit | A structural swing or a fixed reward multiple |
| **Trade filters** | Conditions that permit or block an entry | Session, spread, volatility, news window |
| **Exit management** | What happens after entry | Fixed SL/TP, trailing stop, break-even, time exit |

**ATR measures recent price volatility.** Any ATR-based condition should use information available before the decision. Definitions, confirmation delays, entries, and exits must remain consistent throughout a test. [Order-block research guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)

**Some variables affect exposure rather than the quality of the signal.**

Lot size, leverage, and risk per trade can dramatically increase historical profit **and losses**. Increasing them does not make the entry prediction more accurate. We should compare strategy settings using consistent position sizing.

Spread, commission, and slippage are execution assumptions. We must model them realistically. Exness buys execute at Ask and sells at Bid; using one candle price for everything can distort results. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)

**For the first experiment, I would change only four variables.**

Keep H1 context, M5 triggers, completed-candle rules, ATR period 14, entry method, target rule, and position sizing fixed.

| Variable | Three experimental values |
|---|---|
| Swing confirmation length | 2 / 3 / 5 candles per side |
| Confirmation body Ã· prior completed M5 ATR | 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 |
| Confirmation expiry after sweep | 3 / 6 / 12 M5 candles |
| Stop buffer beyond sweep extreme | 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 Ã— ATR |

That gives **3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 = 81 combinations**. We should also compare the displacement filter against a baseline without it.

**How we decide whether an improvement is credible:**

1. Develop settings on the earliest data.
2. Compare candidates on later validation data.
3. Freeze the selected rules before evaluating an untouched final period.
4. Record every attempted configuration, including failures.
5. Check nearby settings: a broad region of acceptable results is more reassuring than one exceptional combination.
6. Forward-test the frozen version on demo.

Repeatedly searching the same history increases the chance of selecting noise. Even a holdout becomes compromised if we keep using its results to redesign the strategy. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)

We should measure **net expectancy, profit factor, drawdown, trade count, exposure, and stability across periods**. No parameter combination can be called profitable for our app yetâ€”we have verified the trading bridge, but have not backtested this SMC strategy.



