original_answer_variant | ASSISTANT | 2026-10-15T08:22:37.819Z
========================================================================
**Buy when bullish context, a completed bullish trigger, and acceptable trade economics agree. Sell when the bearish equivalents agree. Otherwise, Wait.**

The research supports turning SMC into a **sequence of conditions**. An order block, FVG, sweep, or CHoCH by itself does not establish an entry.

SMC also has no single standardized strategy. Below I distinguish common trading practice from **a proposed rule set for our app**, whose profitability still needs testing.

**What the research actually supports**

Research from the New York Fed found that currency stop-loss and take-profit orders can cluster around certain levels. Crucially, triggering stops can **accelerate an existing trend**. Therefore, â€œprice crossed yesterdayâ€™s high, so sellâ€ is unreliable reasoning: the move might continue. These historical FX findings do not directly validate an SMC strategy on Exness gold. [Order clustering research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr125.html), [stop-loss cascades](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)

Practitioner sources describe a common reversal sequence: **sweep a level â†’ reclaim it â†’ break structure in the opposite direction â†’ enter using a defined confirmation or retracement rule**. This is a trading hypothesis, not proof of a profitable edge. [FXOpenâ€™s explanation](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/)

Candles cannot establish which institution traded, whether hidden orders remain, or whether someone deliberately manipulated price. We should label these as **inferred liquidity levels and candidate reaction zones**. [Order-block definitions and limitations](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)

**When would we consider a Buy?**

One coherent SMC approach is to look for the end of a small downward pullback within a broader upward trend.

| Check | What we need to observe | Decision before it happens |
|---|---|---|
| **1. Broader direction** | The higher timeframe has confirmed higher highs and higher lows. | Wait if direction is unclear. |
| **2. Pullback location** | Price returns to a predefined area of interest, rather than entering after a large upward move. | Wait for price to arrive. |
| **3. Downside sweep and reclaim** | Price trades below an already identified local low, then closes back above it. | A break below alone means Wait. |
| **4. Bullish structure shift** | A later completed candle closes above a relevant, previously known local swing high. | A wick through that high is insufficient under a close-based rule. |
| **5. Entry condition** | The selected entry model completes: confirmation entry **or** a specified retracement/rejection entry. | Do not switch models halfway through. |
| **6. Stop and target** | A defensible stop and a target identified before entry leave sufficient potential reward after costs. | Wait or reject if they do not. |

The higher timeframe can remain bullish while the lower-timeframe pullback is bearish. A bullish CHoCH on the lower timeframe can therefore signal the **possible end of that pullback**, without implying a brand-new higher-timeframe uptrend. BOS and CHoCH must always be tied to the structure being measured. [Structure concepts](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)

**When would we consider a Sell?**

Use the exact opposite sequence:

1. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish.
2. Price rallies into a predefined area of interest.
3. It moves above an already identified local high and closes back below.
4. A later completed candle closes below the relevant local swing low.
5. The chosen entry condition completes.
6. A stop above the invalidation area and a predefined downside target leave sufficient potential reward after costs.

**A bullish market does not become a Sell simply because price looks expensive.** Likewise, a bearish market does not become a Buy simply because price has fallen substantially.

**Continuation and reversal need separate rules**

Two common SMC approaches should be evaluated separately:

| Model | Typical sequence |
|---|---|
| **Trend continuation** | Established trend â†’ BOS with that trend â†’ retracement into the breaking moveâ€™s candidate order block â†’ selected entry trigger. |
| **Sweep and structure shift** | Cross a known high/low â†’ reclaim it â†’ opposite structure break â†’ selected entry trigger. |

A sweep-and-shift setup can occur inside a higher-timeframe pullback or against the broader trend. Those are different experiments; we should record them separately.

There are also two different entry methods:

- **Confirmation entry:** enter after the required reaction or structure break completes. It enters later and may leave less reward relative to the stop.
- **Retracement entry:** wait for price to revisit a predetermined level or zone. It may offer a better entry price, but can remain unfilled or enter while the zone fails.

Neither method is universally superior. They require separate tests using the same original setup definitions. [Entry-method trade-offs](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)

**When should the app say Wait?**

Wait should be an explicit, explainable result.

| Situation | App explanation |
|---|---|
| Structure is mixed or ranging | **WAIT â€” no directional context** |
| Price only touches an order block/FVG | **WAIT â€” entry trigger incomplete** |
| Price crosses a high/low but does not reclaim it | **WAIT â€” sweep/rejection unconfirmed** |
| Reclaim occurs without the required structure break | **WAIT â€” structure confirmation missing** |
| The model requires a retracement and none occurs | **WAIT â€” awaiting retracement** |
| Price has already moved close to the target | **REJECT â€” insufficient remaining reward** |
| The zone or setupâ€™s invalidation boundary is breached | **CANCEL â€” setup invalidated** |
| Confirmation takes too long | **CANCEL â€” setup expired** |
| Spread, quotes, market permissions, or data fail checks | **BLOCK â€” execution conditions unsuitable** |
| Position or exposure limits are reached | **BLOCK â€” risk limit reached** |

A news exclusion window can also be tested, but its timing must be defined in advance rather than added after observing losing trades.

**My proposed first experiment for MarketLink**

To start with manageable rules, I would test the following **demo research model**. These settings are design choices, not proven optimal values.

| Element | Initial experimental definition |
|---|---|
| Context timeframe | H1 |
| Trigger timeframe | M5 |
| Swing detection | A pivot with two completed candles on each side |
| Bullish/bearish context | Latest two confirmed highs **and** lows both rising/falling; otherwise Wait |
| Sweep | Cross a local level known before the candle began, then close back inside |
| Confirmation | A subsequent candle closes beyond the opposite pre-sweep swing |
| Confirmation expiry | Six M5 candles |
| Entry baseline | First tradable quote after confirmation completes |
| Stop | Beyond the sweep extreme, with a predefined buffer |
| Target | A relevant opposing swing selected and frozen before entry |
| Reward/risk gate | Test a minimum **2:1 after estimated costs** |
| Position handling | One position; fixed initial SL/TP; one attempt per setup |

For this baseline, order-block and FVG retracement entries would be **separate later variants**. That lets us measure whether each addition improves results.

Swing timing matters: a two-right-candle pivot becomes known only after those later candles close. A historical label placed on the pivot candle must not allow the backtest to enter there earlier. [MetaQuotesâ€™ five-bar fractal definition](https://www.mql5.com/en/code/30)

**Why entry price can change Buy into Wait**

Consider hypothetical prices, before costs:

- Entry **100**, stop **98**, target **105**: potential reward/risk is **5 Ã· 2 = 2.5:1**.
- If entry is delayed until **104**, with the same stop and target: **1 Ã· 6 â‰ˆ 0.17:1**.

The direction might still be bullish, but the second trade fails the proposed entry gate. We should not move the target farther away merely to manufacture an attractive ratio.

A 2:1 target also does not guarantee profitability; the realized win rate, losses, slippage, and costs determine expectancy.

**What matters specifically for your MT5 bridge**

Exness documents that a Buy opens at **Ask** and closes at **Bid**, while a Sell opens at **Bid** and closes at **Ask**. Backtests must use those executable prices, spread, commission, and slippage. A chart-only touch is insufficient to assume a fill. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)

The app should eventually display the **current decision, completed conditions, missing condition, invalidation level, target, and signal time**. We should avoid displaying an invented â€œ90% confidenceâ€ score.

Before enabling strategy-driven orders, freeze the rules, evaluate them chronologically on unseen data, record every attempted variant, and forward-test on demo. Repeatedly choosing the best-looking backtest can produce a convincing strategy with no durable edge. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)

