{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 14, "timestamp": "2026-10-12T19:21:32.522Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd879be6a88191ad4327117ffbb5a9", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Iâ€™ll research the open-source projects, market-learning approaches, and faster tick-backtesting options, then build a roadmap around what we can realistically test. Iâ€™ll also check your earlier XAUUSD work so the plan carries forward useful lessons.\n"], "text": "Iâ€™ll research the open-source projects, market-learning approaches, and faster tick-backtesting options, then build a roadmap around what we can realistically test. Iâ€™ll also check your earlier XAUUSD work so the plan carries forward useful lessons.\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 2, "text_sha256": "449e499bf10e273124eef7404f3ee4ac8983f8c391df52c5734cf437684dd73d", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 54, "timestamp": "2026-10-12T19:22:54.511Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd87eb75d881918bd4adeb642785f7", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["The research points to a useful structure: an AI researcher proposes and reviews ideas, while a numerical engine tests them quickly and MT5 validates the strongest candidates.\n\nThereâ€™s also a data limit to account for: Exness bid/ask ticks can support studies of spreads, volatility, and sweep/reversal patterns. They cannot directly reveal hidden orders or prove manipulation.\n"], "text": "The research points to a useful structure: an AI researcher proposes and reviews ideas, while a numerical engine tests them quickly and MT5 validates the strongest candidates.\n\nThereâ€™s also a data limit to account for: Exness bid/ask ticks can support studies of spreads, volatility, and sweep/reversal patterns. They cannot directly reveal hidden orders or prove manipulation.\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 3, "text_sha256": "d3909198879bfcf06bf3c7276ed4e397038b91c99b0ac31f4e6e7ab99aa757e6", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 84, "timestamp": "2026-10-12T19:28:55.491Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd895572d88191b085f0eff8fc156f", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Understoodâ€”you want an adaptable system that learns across markets, with a path to live trading. Iâ€™ll make market coverage and learning capability central to the research, and treat hardware as a later sizing decision.\n\nThe closest projects cover different parts of that goal: automated strategy discovery, experience memory, chart reasoning, and neural market models. Iâ€™m checking how each was evaluated and which parts could transfer to MT5.\n"], "text": "Understoodâ€”you want an adaptable system that learns across markets, with a path to live trading. Iâ€™ll make market coverage and learning capability central to the research, and treat hardware as a later sizing decision.\n\nThe closest projects cover different parts of that goal: automated strategy discovery, experience memory, chart reasoning, and neural market models. Iâ€™m checking how each was evaluated and which parts could transfer to MT5.\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 4, "text_sha256": "691a6d184bd5a3868407d5c1bc39c32148ca514c8404f39d21ba335b8b2d4b64", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 150, "timestamp": "2026-10-12T19:32:26.145Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd8a2863c481919819d4d52572f231", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Two especially relevant directions have emerged: Microsoftâ€™s RD-Agent for automated research, and Kronos for learned financial chart representations. FinAgent and FinMem are useful references for experience memory.\n\nBroader evaluations also matter. The FINSABER paper found that reported LLM trading advantages weakened substantially across longer periods and more symbols. The roadmap will therefore test both strategy performance and whether learned experience transfers to unseen markets.\n"], "text": "Two especially relevant directions have emerged: Microsoftâ€™s RD-Agent for automated research, and Kronos for learned financial chart representations. FinAgent and FinMem are useful references for experience memory.\n\nBroader evaluations also matter. The FINSABER paper found that reported LLM trading advantages weakened substantially across longer periods and more symbols. The roadmap will therefore test both strategy performance and whether learned experience transfers to unseen markets.\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 5, "text_sha256": "42a9d71dfc3a89a8011a9fc48f9ec07aa79c22be21b0cdbd425c5bbb7fbf3065", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 207, "timestamp": "2026-10-12T19:35:15.739Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd8ad23b3881919727410270b4bb92", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["The roadmap now has two measures of progress: a trustworthy research engine, and demonstrated learning across markets. A good backtest on one instrument would establish neither transfer nor adaptation.\n\nIâ€™m organizing the plan around your choices: full market context, several decision time scales, shared learning followed by market-specific adaptation, and human approval before a new version controls live trades.\n"], "text": "The roadmap now has two measures of progress: a trustworthy research engine, and demonstrated learning across markets. A good backtest on one instrument would establish neither transfer nor adaptation.\n\nIâ€™m organizing the plan around your choices: full market context, several decision time scales, shared learning followed by market-specific adaptation, and human approval before a new version controls live trades.\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 6, "text_sha256": "cf71f4380489733867ddb2eb4f44b86da2d898c3dc4b79930d4fd3faef78b8ac", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 227, "timestamp": "2026-10-13T01:38:48.112Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016acd8b23abb8819197fd29ab1fe59587", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["<proposed_plan>\n# Market-Learning AI: Research Map and Development Roadmap\n\n## 1. The goal and the research direction\n\nYour goal is a system that **learns useful market behavior, remembers experience, adapts to unfamiliar markets, decides when to trade or skip, and eventually executes validated decisions**.\n\nBased on your choices, the design should support:\n\n- Multiple markets, with shared learning followed by market-specific adaptation.\n- Ticks, charts, news, economic events, and relationships between markets.\n- Decisions across several time scales.\n- Continuous research, followed by validation and human approval before deployment.\n- Hardware selected after measuring the workload.\n\n**My recommendation is a hybrid learning system:** neural models learn numerical market representations; experience memory stores earlier situations and outcomes; an LLM conducts research and interprets evidence; a numerical simulator evaluates decisions.\n\nThese are different forms of learning:\n\n| Mechanism | What changes |\n|---|---|\n| Neural training | Model weights change through examples and objectives. |\n| Experience memory | The system stores and retrieves earlier observations, decisions, and outcomes. |\n| Strategy discovery | The system proposes, tests, rejects, and improves trading rules or models. |\n\nA strong application can combine all three. Whether it develops transferable trading skill must be demonstrated experimentally. **None of the projects reviewed establishes reliable profitability across arbitrary markets.**\n\nThe research below was checked against primary repositories, documentation, and papers on **13 October 2026**. It is a curated starting map; I have not installed these projects or reproduced their results.\n\n## 2. Existing work most relevant to your idea\n\n**Automated research and strategy discovery**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Microsoft RD-Agent](https://github.com/microsoft/RD-Agent) + [Qlib](https://github.com/microsoft/qlib)** | The closest starting reference for an AI researcher: propose factors/models, implement experiments, examine feedback, and improve. Qlib supplies numerical research infrastructure. | Existing financial examples do not reproduce Exness tick execution. RD-Agent currently documents Linux support, so Windows deployment needs a separate research environment or a lighter implementation of its workflow. |\n| **[AlphaGen](https://github.com/ICT-FinD-Lab/alphagen)** | Trains a neural policy to construct useful mathematical trading factors. Relevant to searching beyond manually selected indicator parameters. | Its neural network learns formula construction. Its published task is equity factor discovery, rather than a general market world model. |\n| **[AlphaAgent](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16789)** | Separates market hypotheses, factor generation, and evaluation, with controls against redundant or overly complex factors. | The paper implementation is preserved in the repositoryâ€™s [`legacy-main` branch](https://github.com/RndmVariableQ/AlphaAgent/tree/legacy-main). Current `main` has changed substantially. |\n| **[QuantaAlpha](https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/QuantaAlpha)** | Evolves research trajectories through reflection, mutation, and recombination. Useful for remembering which research paths worked or failed. | Its [latest paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07085v3) studies stock-factor research. â€œSelf-evolvingâ€ principally describes the research process; it does not mean the LLM continuously retrains itself from ticks. |\n\n**Learning market patterns and representations**\n\n| Project or research | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Kronos](https://github.com/shiyu-coder/Kronos)** | A financial foundation model trained on candlestick sequences from many exchanges. Particularly relevant to your shared-learning ambition. | It models candles, rather than broker tick execution. Its authors explicitly describe the example trading pipeline as simplified. Forecasting ability still needs economic validation after costs. |\n| **[Chronos](https://github.com/amazon-science/chronos-forecasting)** | Pretrained time-series models that provide useful forecasting baselines. | General forecasting results do not establish trading profitability. Test against simpler models on the same market data. |\n| **[TS2Vec](https://github.com/zhihanyue/ts2vec)** | Learns representations of time-series segments. Useful for recognizing similar market situations and retrieving previous experiences. | Similar-looking sequences need not have similar future outcomes. That relationship must be tested. |\n| **[DeepLOB](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.03668)** | Neural learning from order-book dynamics. Relevant to understanding richer microstructure inputs. | Its input contains substantially more information than ordinary Exness bid/ask ticks. |\n| **[Universal features of price formation](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.06917)** | Evidence that pooled training across stocks can improve prediction, including on stocks excluded from training. | This supports researching transfer. It does not establish that a model trained on equities transfers successfully to every asset class. |\n\n**Experience, chart reasoning, and financial agents**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[FinAgent](https://github.com/DVampire/FinAgent)** | Combines numerical information, charts, text, memory, and reflection. One of the closest conceptual matches to learning from trading experience. | Published evaluation uses daily market information and a limited asset set, rather than Exness tick replay. |\n| **[FinMem](https://github.com/pipiku915/FinMem-LLM-StockTrading)** | Layered memory and retrieval inspired by human memory. Useful for separating recent events from longer-lasting lessons. | Retrieving memories is different from updating neural weights. Its documented embedding setup also needs modification for fully local operation. |\n| **[TradingAgents](https://github.com/TauricResearch/TradingAgents)** | Specialized analysts, opposing arguments, trading decisions, and risk review. Useful for structured critique of hypotheses. | Multiple agents can share the same incorrect assumptions. Debate needs independent numerical evidence. |\n| **[QuantHarness](https://github.com/Y-Research-SBU/QuantHarness)** | Indicator, chart-pattern, and trend analysis with LLM/vision agents. | The [latest paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09995v4) evaluates hourly candles and reports limitations at shorter intervals. It does not demonstrate tick-speed decision making. |\n| **[FinGPT](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinGPT)** | Financial language processing, sentiment, datasets, and model adaptation. Useful for a future news/context module. | Understanding financial language does not automatically provide skill in numerical market prediction. |\n| **[FinRobot](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinRobot)** | Agent workflows, financial tools, provenance, and reports. Its separation of computed numbers from LLM explanations is valuable. | Its current emphasis is equity research and valuation, rather than high-throughput broker-tick strategy discovery. |\n\n**Adaptive systems, reinforcement learning, and simulation**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Freqtrade/FreqAI](https://www.freqtrade.io/en/stable/freqai/)** | Rolling model retraining, historical simulation of adaptation, and operational trading workflows. | Mainly crypto/exchange infrastructure; adapting its assumptions to MT5 CFDs requires substantial work. |\n| **[FinRL](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinRL)** | Financial reinforcement-learning environments and training workflows. | An RL agent can exploit errors in its simulator. A trusted execution model must come first. |\n| **[FinWorld](https://github.com/DVampire/FinWorld)** | Broad financial research architecture covering ML, neural models, RL, LLMs, and heterogeneous data. | The repository explicitly labels itself a preview with potentially incomplete components. It is a research platform, rather than proof of universal market intelligence. |\n| **[ABIDES](https://github.com/abides-sim/abides)** | A synthetic exchange with interacting agents, messages, and latency. Useful for controlled experiments in market mechanics. | Synthetic market behavior must be validated before drawing conclusions about real venues. |\n| **[DreamerV3](https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3)** | A reference for learning environment dynamics and planning through imagined outcomes. | Its general-control success does not establish financial applicability. Treat market world models as a later research branch. |\n\n**Faster backtesting foundations**\n\n| Engine | Recommended role |\n|---|---|\n| **[NautilusTrader](https://github.com/nautechsystems/nautilus_trader)** | Leading candidate for detailed event-driven quote replay, with a compiled Rust core. Requires an MT5 adapter and calibrated execution/accounting models. |\n| **[LEAN](https://github.com/QuantConnect/Lean)** | Alternative for broader multi-asset infrastructure. Distinguish its open-source engine from the official [CLIâ€™s current paid-organization requirement](https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/lean-cli/key-concepts/getting-started). |\n| **[hftbacktest](https://github.com/nkaz001/hftbacktest)** | Valuable when genuine trade and order-book data are available. Its queue/depth modeling benefits cannot be recovered from bid/ask prices alone. |\n| **[vectorbt](https://github.com/polakowo/vectorbt)** | Useful reference for vectorized parameter screening. Its [current license includes Commons Clause](https://github.com/polakowo/vectorbt/blob/master/LICENSE.md), which needs review before product reuse. |\n\nBefore copying code, audit the exact versionâ€™s license, dependencies, model weights, and dataset rights. Publicly readable research code is not always clearly licensed for reuse; AlphaGen and QuantaAlpha need particular clarification.\n\n**Evaluation research is essential reading.** [FINSABER](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.07078) reevaluated LLM investing strategies over two decades and more than 100 symbols, finding that previously reported advantages deteriorated substantially under broader evaluation. Its lesson for this project is to measure generalization before adding more agent complexity.\n\nMy suggested reading order is **RD-Agent/Qlib â†’ Kronos â†’ FinAgent/FinMem â†’ FINSABER â†’ NautilusTrader**, followed by the specialized branches above.\n\n## 3. Architecture and development roadmap\n\nThe application should have a market-independent research core, with separate adapters for each data source and trading venue.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Ticks, charts, news, economic events] --> B[Timestamped datasets and market adapters]\n    B --> C[Shared models and experience memory]\n    C --> D[Proposed trading decisions]\n    D --> E[Independent risk controls]\n    E --> F[Demo or approved live execution]\n\n    G[AI researcher] --> H[Fast numerical experiments]\n    H --> I[Detailed tick replay and platform checks]\n    I --> J[Evidence and failure records]\n    J --> G\n    J --> K[Human approval]\n    K --> C\n```\n\n**What â€œlearning from experienceâ€ should mean**\n\nFor example, the system observes a break above an already-known session high. It combines spread behavior, volatility, cross-market context, and comparable earlier episodes. It estimates possible outcomes and costs, then chooses trade or skip. After the outcome becomes available, it records the result and updates through a declared learning procedure.\n\nEach part needs an experiment: does memory help, does context help, does adaptation help, and does the resulting decision survive realistic execution?\n\n**Core interfaces**\n\n- **Market observations:** original data, instrument/venue identity, event time, and when the information became available.\n- **Instrument specifications:** contract value, price/lot increments, currencies, sessions, fees, financing, margin, and order rules.\n- **Experiment specifications:** frozen hypothesis, model/rules, data versions, chronological splits, execution assumptions, and search budget.\n- **Evidence reports:** trades, accounting, uncertainty, failures, compute usage, and every attempted configuration.\n- **Approved policies:** immutable model/rule versions consumed by execution after human approval.\n\n**Development sequence**\n\n| Stage | Work | Evidence required to advance |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Research dossier** | Classify the projects above, inspect pinned code, audit datasets/licenses, and select reproducible examples. | A comparison distinguishing claimed capabilities, implemented capabilities, and reproduced evidence. |\n| **2. Multi-market data foundation** | Build the first Exness/MT5 adapter; preserve raw exports; create validated Parquet datasets and causal features. Cover several asset groups from the beginning. | Reproducible exports, documented coverage, correct timestamps, and validated contract metadata. |\n| **3. Trusted simulation** | Build compiled screening and detailed quote replay; reconcile fixed strategies against MT5. | Explained signal, fill, cost, and accounting differences; measured speed and memory usage. |\n| **4. Learned market representations** | Compare simple rules, statistical models, boosted trees, sequence encoders, and suitable foundation models. | Improvement on later periods and held-out instruments under equal evaluation conditions. |\n| **5. Experience and research agent** | Add episode retrieval, failure memory, bounded hypothesis generation, and experiment orchestration. | Measurable value over the same system without memory or LLM research. |\n| **6. Multimodal transfer** | Add charts, news, economic releases, cross-market context, and fixed adaptation procedures. | Benefits survive availability-time checks and unfamiliar-market tests. |\n| **7. Demo and deployment** | Run approved versions prospectively; compare expected and observed execution; introduce live operation through explicit promotion gates. | Reliable reconciliation, operational fault tests, and independently enforced risk limits. |\n| **8. Advanced research** | Investigate RL, learned world models, planning, and continual learning. | Added value over simpler validated methods, including tests for harmful adaptation and forgotten skills. |\n\n**How to make backtesting faster**\n\nUse three levels:\n\n1. **Broad screening:** cached causal features, vectorized/JIT calculations, bounded parallel jobs, and early rejection.\n2. **Detailed replay:** every relevant bid/ask update for shortlisted candidates, including realistic order timing and costs.\n3. **Platform verification:** frozen candidates tested against native MT5 behavior.\n\nStrategies whose logic depends on individual ticks must retain those events during screening. Simplification must be explicit.\n\nBenchmark identical strategies, intervals, and execution assumptions. Record cold loading, warm runs, compilation, worker count, peak RAM, and trade differences. A faster result is useful only when its fidelity is understood.\n\nFor MT5 ingestion, use [`copy_ticks_range`](https://www.mql5.com/en/docs/python_metatrader5/mt5copyticksrange_py), preserving UTC timestamps, millisecond fields, flags, and original event order. Selecting real-tick tester mode alone is insufficient: MetaQuotes documents [generated fallback when tick history is missing](https://www.metatrader5.com/en/terminal/help/algotrading/tick_generation).\n\nExnessâ€™s downloadable archive is a separate source: its [documentation identifies particular MT4 servers](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360021547851-Tick-history). Keep connected-MT5 exports canonical and label other archives separately.\n\nOrdinary quote ticks also lack executable depth. Nautilus requires [bid/ask sizes](https://nautilustrader.io/docs/latest/concepts/data/quote_tick/); any synthetic-size assumption must remain explicit.\n\n## 4. Tests that establish intelligence and trading reliability\n\n**Cross-market learning**\n\nCompare:\n\n- A shared frozen model.\n- The shared model after a fixed adaptation procedure.\n- A market-specific model trained with the same target-data budget.\n- Simple rules and numerical baselines.\n\nSeparate tests for future dates, unseen instruments, unseen venues, and unseen asset classes. Source-market training must also respect the chronological boundary. Report individual markets so a strong aggregate cannot hide failed deployment domains.\n\n**Experience and adaptation**\n\n- Retrieved episodes must precede the decision being evaluated.\n- Outcomes enter memory or training only after they become available.\n- Compare adaptation against a frozen model.\n- Recheck older tasks after adaptation to measure forgetting.\n- Measure whether confidence improves trade/skip decisions.\n- Test whether explanations refer to observations that actually influenced the decision.\n\n**Leakage and selection**\n\n- Appending future observations must not change earlier features or decisions.\n- Use calendar-time splits for irregular ticks and remove training labels whose outcome windows cross evaluation boundaries.\n- Fit preprocessing and feature selection inside each training period.\n- Keep final evaluation inaccessible to research agents.\n- Record unsuccessful trials, seeds, parameter changes, and abandoned hypotheses.\n- Freeze the campaignâ€™s search budget and promotion criteria before searching.\n\nRepeated experiments can select luck. Use the [backtest-overfitting research](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf) alongside chronological and prospective evaluation.\n\nHistorical LLM tests also need model-training provenance: hiding future observations from a prompt does not remove knowledge already stored in weights. [DatedGPT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11838) studies this problem directly. Prospective predictions recorded before outcomes exist provide particularly useful evidence.\n\n**Economic and execution correctness**\n\nTest bid/ask sides, commissions, financing, currency conversion, position accounting, gaps, delayed fills, margin, and portfolio exposure. Add asset-specific corporate actions, expiry, or settlement when those markets are introduced.\n\nReport net returns, drawdown, profit factor, turnover, exposure, uncertainty, and results by market and period. Stress spreads, latency, slippage, and nearby parameter values.\n\nQuote patterns may support a measurable â€œsweep and reversalâ€ hypothesis. They cannot by themselves establish hidden stop locations or manipulative intent; that limitation follows from the documented [Exness feed contents](https://www.exness.com/tick-history/).\n\n**Operational deployment**\n\nBefore live promotion, demonstrate:\n\n- Duplicate-order prevention and broker/account reconciliation.\n- Safe recovery from disconnects, rejected orders, crashes, and stale data.\n- Independent exposure and loss limits.\n- A visible hard stop and rollback to the previous approved version.\n- No automatic modification of live policies by the research agent.\n\n## 5. The concrete starting milestone\n\nStart with a **research and feasibility package** containing four deliverables:\n\n1. **Evidence matrix:** project, paper/version, learning mechanism, inputs, markets, time scale, code/weights availability, license, dependencies, evaluation weaknesses, and reusable components.\n2. **Architecture specification:** the interfaces above, data availability rules, simulator boundaries, and model promotion workflow.\n3. **Three reproducible pilot studies:**\n   - MT5 versus external replay: correctness, speed, and resource use.\n   - Shared learning versus market-specific learning across several asset groups.\n   - Numerical search versus LLM-guided research under equal experiment budgets.\n4. **Decision report:** which components to adopt, which to implement independently, what failed, and the measured hardware requirements.\n\nUse Python for research orchestration, partitioned Parquet/DuckDB for data, PyTorch for neural experiments, compiled numerical routines for screening, and an isolated Windows MT5 bridge. Evaluate NautilusTrader first for detailed replay; retain the simulator interface so the engine can be replaced.\n\nThe first interface should expose datasets, experiments, models/memory, replay evidence, and approvals. Build around observable research work rather than a chat window alone.\n\nYour current decisions supersede the earlier single-pair and hardware constraints. The first adapter is Exness/MT5; additional markets require validated data, accounting, and execution adapters. Hardware purchasing follows benchmark results.\n\n**The first success is a reproducible system that can demonstrateâ€”or rejectâ€”learning and transfer.** Zero qualifying strategies remains a valid research result. Live trading is a later deployment milestone, after the evidence supports it.\n</proposed_plan>\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-237|note=[prior architecture and validation lessons]\nrollout_summaries/2026-09-26T17-48-16-Ok1N-xauusd_local_ai_research_handbook_pdf.md:23-28|note=[separation of research learning and numerical evaluation]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "<proposed_plan>\n# Market-Learning AI: Research Map and Development Roadmap\n\n## 1. The goal and the research direction\n\nYour goal is a system that **learns useful market behavior, remembers experience, adapts to unfamiliar markets, decides when to trade or skip, and eventually executes validated decisions**.\n\nBased on your choices, the design should support:\n\n- Multiple markets, with shared learning followed by market-specific adaptation.\n- Ticks, charts, news, economic events, and relationships between markets.\n- Decisions across several time scales.\n- Continuous research, followed by validation and human approval before deployment.\n- Hardware selected after measuring the workload.\n\n**My recommendation is a hybrid learning system:** neural models learn numerical market representations; experience memory stores earlier situations and outcomes; an LLM conducts research and interprets evidence; a numerical simulator evaluates decisions.\n\nThese are different forms of learning:\n\n| Mechanism | What changes |\n|---|---|\n| Neural training | Model weights change through examples and objectives. |\n| Experience memory | The system stores and retrieves earlier observations, decisions, and outcomes. |\n| Strategy discovery | The system proposes, tests, rejects, and improves trading rules or models. |\n\nA strong application can combine all three. Whether it develops transferable trading skill must be demonstrated experimentally. **None of the projects reviewed establishes reliable profitability across arbitrary markets.**\n\nThe research below was checked against primary repositories, documentation, and papers on **13 October 2026**. It is a curated starting map; I have not installed these projects or reproduced their results.\n\n## 2. Existing work most relevant to your idea\n\n**Automated research and strategy discovery**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Microsoft RD-Agent](https://github.com/microsoft/RD-Agent) + [Qlib](https://github.com/microsoft/qlib)** | The closest starting reference for an AI researcher: propose factors/models, implement experiments, examine feedback, and improve. Qlib supplies numerical research infrastructure. | Existing financial examples do not reproduce Exness tick execution. RD-Agent currently documents Linux support, so Windows deployment needs a separate research environment or a lighter implementation of its workflow. |\n| **[AlphaGen](https://github.com/ICT-FinD-Lab/alphagen)** | Trains a neural policy to construct useful mathematical trading factors. Relevant to searching beyond manually selected indicator parameters. | Its neural network learns formula construction. Its published task is equity factor discovery, rather than a general market world model. |\n| **[AlphaAgent](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.16789)** | Separates market hypotheses, factor generation, and evaluation, with controls against redundant or overly complex factors. | The paper implementation is preserved in the repositoryâ€™s [`legacy-main` branch](https://github.com/RndmVariableQ/AlphaAgent/tree/legacy-main). Current `main` has changed substantially. |\n| **[QuantaAlpha](https://github.com/QuantaAlpha/QuantaAlpha)** | Evolves research trajectories through reflection, mutation, and recombination. Useful for remembering which research paths worked or failed. | Its [latest paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07085v3) studies stock-factor research. â€œSelf-evolvingâ€ principally describes the research process; it does not mean the LLM continuously retrains itself from ticks. |\n\n**Learning market patterns and representations**\n\n| Project or research | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Kronos](https://github.com/shiyu-coder/Kronos)** | A financial foundation model trained on candlestick sequences from many exchanges. Particularly relevant to your shared-learning ambition. | It models candles, rather than broker tick execution. Its authors explicitly describe the example trading pipeline as simplified. Forecasting ability still needs economic validation after costs. |\n| **[Chronos](https://github.com/amazon-science/chronos-forecasting)** | Pretrained time-series models that provide useful forecasting baselines. | General forecasting results do not establish trading profitability. Test against simpler models on the same market data. |\n| **[TS2Vec](https://github.com/zhihanyue/ts2vec)** | Learns representations of time-series segments. Useful for recognizing similar market situations and retrieving previous experiences. | Similar-looking sequences need not have similar future outcomes. That relationship must be tested. |\n| **[DeepLOB](https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.03668)** | Neural learning from order-book dynamics. Relevant to understanding richer microstructure inputs. | Its input contains substantially more information than ordinary Exness bid/ask ticks. |\n| **[Universal features of price formation](https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.06917)** | Evidence that pooled training across stocks can improve prediction, including on stocks excluded from training. | This supports researching transfer. It does not establish that a model trained on equities transfers successfully to every asset class. |\n\n**Experience, chart reasoning, and financial agents**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[FinAgent](https://github.com/DVampire/FinAgent)** | Combines numerical information, charts, text, memory, and reflection. One of the closest conceptual matches to learning from trading experience. | Published evaluation uses daily market information and a limited asset set, rather than Exness tick replay. |\n| **[FinMem](https://github.com/pipiku915/FinMem-LLM-StockTrading)** | Layered memory and retrieval inspired by human memory. Useful for separating recent events from longer-lasting lessons. | Retrieving memories is different from updating neural weights. Its documented embedding setup also needs modification for fully local operation. |\n| **[TradingAgents](https://github.com/TauricResearch/TradingAgents)** | Specialized analysts, opposing arguments, trading decisions, and risk review. Useful for structured critique of hypotheses. | Multiple agents can share the same incorrect assumptions. Debate needs independent numerical evidence. |\n| **[QuantHarness](https://github.com/Y-Research-SBU/QuantHarness)** | Indicator, chart-pattern, and trend analysis with LLM/vision agents. | The [latest paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.09995v4) evaluates hourly candles and reports limitations at shorter intervals. It does not demonstrate tick-speed decision making. |\n| **[FinGPT](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinGPT)** | Financial language processing, sentiment, datasets, and model adaptation. Useful for a future news/context module. | Understanding financial language does not automatically provide skill in numerical market prediction. |\n| **[FinRobot](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinRobot)** | Agent workflows, financial tools, provenance, and reports. Its separation of computed numbers from LLM explanations is valuable. | Its current emphasis is equity research and valuation, rather than high-throughput broker-tick strategy discovery. |\n\n**Adaptive systems, reinforcement learning, and simulation**\n\n| Project | What it contributes | Boundary to understand |\n|---|---|---|\n| **[Freqtrade/FreqAI](https://www.freqtrade.io/en/stable/freqai/)** | Rolling model retraining, historical simulation of adaptation, and operational trading workflows. | Mainly crypto/exchange infrastructure; adapting its assumptions to MT5 CFDs requires substantial work. |\n| **[FinRL](https://github.com/AI4Finance-Foundation/FinRL)** | Financial reinforcement-learning environments and training workflows. | An RL agent can exploit errors in its simulator. A trusted execution model must come first. |\n| **[FinWorld](https://github.com/DVampire/FinWorld)** | Broad financial research architecture covering ML, neural models, RL, LLMs, and heterogeneous data. | The repository explicitly labels itself a preview with potentially incomplete components. It is a research platform, rather than proof of universal market intelligence. |\n| **[ABIDES](https://github.com/abides-sim/abides)** | A synthetic exchange with interacting agents, messages, and latency. Useful for controlled experiments in market mechanics. | Synthetic market behavior must be validated before drawing conclusions about real venues. |\n| **[DreamerV3](https://github.com/danijar/dreamerv3)** | A reference for learning environment dynamics and planning through imagined outcomes. | Its general-control success does not establish financial applicability. Treat market world models as a later research branch. |\n\n**Faster backtesting foundations**\n\n| Engine | Recommended role |\n|---|---|\n| **[NautilusTrader](https://github.com/nautechsystems/nautilus_trader)** | Leading candidate for detailed event-driven quote replay, with a compiled Rust core. Requires an MT5 adapter and calibrated execution/accounting models. |\n| **[LEAN](https://github.com/QuantConnect/Lean)** | Alternative for broader multi-asset infrastructure. Distinguish its open-source engine from the official [CLIâ€™s current paid-organization requirement](https://www.quantconnect.com/docs/v2/lean-cli/key-concepts/getting-started). |\n| **[hftbacktest](https://github.com/nkaz001/hftbacktest)** | Valuable when genuine trade and order-book data are available. Its queue/depth modeling benefits cannot be recovered from bid/ask prices alone. |\n| **[vectorbt](https://github.com/polakowo/vectorbt)** | Useful reference for vectorized parameter screening. Its [current license includes Commons Clause](https://github.com/polakowo/vectorbt/blob/master/LICENSE.md), which needs review before product reuse. |\n\nBefore copying code, audit the exact versionâ€™s license, dependencies, model weights, and dataset rights. Publicly readable research code is not always clearly licensed for reuse; AlphaGen and QuantaAlpha need particular clarification.\n\n**Evaluation research is essential reading.** [FINSABER](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.07078) reevaluated LLM investing strategies over two decades and more than 100 symbols, finding that previously reported advantages deteriorated substantially under broader evaluation. Its lesson for this project is to measure generalization before adding more agent complexity.\n\nMy suggested reading order is **RD-Agent/Qlib â†’ Kronos â†’ FinAgent/FinMem â†’ FINSABER â†’ NautilusTrader**, followed by the specialized branches above.\n\n## 3. Architecture and development roadmap\n\nThe application should have a market-independent research core, with separate adapters for each data source and trading venue.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Ticks, charts, news, economic events] --> B[Timestamped datasets and market adapters]\n    B --> C[Shared models and experience memory]\n    C --> D[Proposed trading decisions]\n    D --> E[Independent risk controls]\n    E --> F[Demo or approved live execution]\n\n    G[AI researcher] --> H[Fast numerical experiments]\n    H --> I[Detailed tick replay and platform checks]\n    I --> J[Evidence and failure records]\n    J --> G\n    J --> K[Human approval]\n    K --> C\n```\n\n**What â€œlearning from experienceâ€ should mean**\n\nFor example, the system observes a break above an already-known session high. It combines spread behavior, volatility, cross-market context, and comparable earlier episodes. It estimates possible outcomes and costs, then chooses trade or skip. After the outcome becomes available, it records the result and updates through a declared learning procedure.\n\nEach part needs an experiment: does memory help, does context help, does adaptation help, and does the resulting decision survive realistic execution?\n\n**Core interfaces**\n\n- **Market observations:** original data, instrument/venue identity, event time, and when the information became available.\n- **Instrument specifications:** contract value, price/lot increments, currencies, sessions, fees, financing, margin, and order rules.\n- **Experiment specifications:** frozen hypothesis, model/rules, data versions, chronological splits, execution assumptions, and search budget.\n- **Evidence reports:** trades, accounting, uncertainty, failures, compute usage, and every attempted configuration.\n- **Approved policies:** immutable model/rule versions consumed by execution after human approval.\n\n**Development sequence**\n\n| Stage | Work | Evidence required to advance |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Research dossier** | Classify the projects above, inspect pinned code, audit datasets/licenses, and select reproducible examples. | A comparison distinguishing claimed capabilities, implemented capabilities, and reproduced evidence. |\n| **2. Multi-market data foundation** | Build the first Exness/MT5 adapter; preserve raw exports; create validated Parquet datasets and causal features. Cover several asset groups from the beginning. | Reproducible exports, documented coverage, correct timestamps, and validated contract metadata. |\n| **3. Trusted simulation** | Build compiled screening and detailed quote replay; reconcile fixed strategies against MT5. | Explained signal, fill, cost, and accounting differences; measured speed and memory usage. |\n| **4. Learned market representations** | Compare simple rules, statistical models, boosted trees, sequence encoders, and suitable foundation models. | Improvement on later periods and held-out instruments under equal evaluation conditions. |\n| **5. Experience and research agent** | Add episode retrieval, failure memory, bounded hypothesis generation, and experiment orchestration. | Measurable value over the same system without memory or LLM research. |\n| **6. Multimodal transfer** | Add charts, news, economic releases, cross-market context, and fixed adaptation procedures. | Benefits survive availability-time checks and unfamiliar-market tests. |\n| **7. Demo and deployment** | Run approved versions prospectively; compare expected and observed execution; introduce live operation through explicit promotion gates. | Reliable reconciliation, operational fault tests, and independently enforced risk limits. |\n| **8. Advanced research** | Investigate RL, learned world models, planning, and continual learning. | Added value over simpler validated methods, including tests for harmful adaptation and forgotten skills. |\n\n**How to make backtesting faster**\n\nUse three levels:\n\n1. **Broad screening:** cached causal features, vectorized/JIT calculations, bounded parallel jobs, and early rejection.\n2. **Detailed replay:** every relevant bid/ask update for shortlisted candidates, including realistic order timing and costs.\n3. **Platform verification:** frozen candidates tested against native MT5 behavior.\n\nStrategies whose logic depends on individual ticks must retain those events during screening. Simplification must be explicit.\n\nBenchmark identical strategies, intervals, and execution assumptions. Record cold loading, warm runs, compilation, worker count, peak RAM, and trade differences. A faster result is useful only when its fidelity is understood.\n\nFor MT5 ingestion, use [`copy_ticks_range`](https://www.mql5.com/en/docs/python_metatrader5/mt5copyticksrange_py), preserving UTC timestamps, millisecond fields, flags, and original event order. Selecting real-tick tester mode alone is insufficient: MetaQuotes documents [generated fallback when tick history is missing](https://www.metatrader5.com/en/terminal/help/algotrading/tick_generation).\n\nExnessâ€™s downloadable archive is a separate source: its [documentation identifies particular MT4 servers](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360021547851-Tick-history). Keep connected-MT5 exports canonical and label other archives separately.\n\nOrdinary quote ticks also lack executable depth. Nautilus requires [bid/ask sizes](https://nautilustrader.io/docs/latest/concepts/data/quote_tick/); any synthetic-size assumption must remain explicit.\n\n## 4. Tests that establish intelligence and trading reliability\n\n**Cross-market learning**\n\nCompare:\n\n- A shared frozen model.\n- The shared model after a fixed adaptation procedure.\n- A market-specific model trained with the same target-data budget.\n- Simple rules and numerical baselines.\n\nSeparate tests for future dates, unseen instruments, unseen venues, and unseen asset classes. Source-market training must also respect the chronological boundary. Report individual markets so a strong aggregate cannot hide failed deployment domains.\n\n**Experience and adaptation**\n\n- Retrieved episodes must precede the decision being evaluated.\n- Outcomes enter memory or training only after they become available.\n- Compare adaptation against a frozen model.\n- Recheck older tasks after adaptation to measure forgetting.\n- Measure whether confidence improves trade/skip decisions.\n- Test whether explanations refer to observations that actually influenced the decision.\n\n**Leakage and selection**\n\n- Appending future observations must not change earlier features or decisions.\n- Use calendar-time splits for irregular ticks and remove training labels whose outcome windows cross evaluation boundaries.\n- Fit preprocessing and feature selection inside each training period.\n- Keep final evaluation inaccessible to research agents.\n- Record unsuccessful trials, seeds, parameter changes, and abandoned hypotheses.\n- Freeze the campaignâ€™s search budget and promotion criteria before searching.\n\nRepeated experiments can select luck. Use the [backtest-overfitting research](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf) alongside chronological and prospective evaluation.\n\nHistorical LLM tests also need model-training provenance: hiding future observations from a prompt does not remove knowledge already stored in weights. [DatedGPT](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.11838) studies this problem directly. Prospective predictions recorded before outcomes exist provide particularly useful evidence.\n\n**Economic and execution correctness**\n\nTest bid/ask sides, commissions, financing, currency conversion, position accounting, gaps, delayed fills, margin, and portfolio exposure. Add asset-specific corporate actions, expiry, or settlement when those markets are introduced.\n\nReport net returns, drawdown, profit factor, turnover, exposure, uncertainty, and results by market and period. Stress spreads, latency, slippage, and nearby parameter values.\n\nQuote patterns may support a measurable â€œsweep and reversalâ€ hypothesis. They cannot by themselves establish hidden stop locations or manipulative intent; that limitation follows from the documented [Exness feed contents](https://www.exness.com/tick-history/).\n\n**Operational deployment**\n\nBefore live promotion, demonstrate:\n\n- Duplicate-order prevention and broker/account reconciliation.\n- Safe recovery from disconnects, rejected orders, crashes, and stale data.\n- Independent exposure and loss limits.\n- A visible hard stop and rollback to the previous approved version.\n- No automatic modification of live policies by the research agent.\n\n## 5. The concrete starting milestone\n\nStart with a **research and feasibility package** containing four deliverables:\n\n1. **Evidence matrix:** project, paper/version, learning mechanism, inputs, markets, time scale, code/weights availability, license, dependencies, evaluation weaknesses, and reusable components.\n2. **Architecture specification:** the interfaces above, data availability rules, simulator boundaries, and model promotion workflow.\n3. **Three reproducible pilot studies:**\n   - MT5 versus external replay: correctness, speed, and resource use.\n   - Shared learning versus market-specific learning across several asset groups.\n   - Numerical search versus LLM-guided research under equal experiment budgets.\n4. **Decision report:** which components to adopt, which to implement independently, what failed, and the measured hardware requirements.\n\nUse Python for research orchestration, partitioned Parquet/DuckDB for data, PyTorch for neural experiments, compiled numerical routines for screening, and an isolated Windows MT5 bridge. Evaluate NautilusTrader first for detailed replay; retain the simulator interface so the engine can be replaced.\n\nThe first interface should expose datasets, experiments, models/memory, replay evidence, and approvals. Build around observable research work rather than a chat window alone.\n\nYour current decisions supersede the earlier single-pair and hardware constraints. The first adapter is Exness/MT5; additional markets require validated data, accounting, and execution adapters. Hardware purchasing follows benchmark results.\n\n**The first success is a reproducible system that can demonstrateâ€”or rejectâ€”learning and transfer.** Zero qualifying strategies remains a valid research result. Live trading is a later deployment milestone, after the evidence supports it.\n</proposed_plan>\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-237|note=[prior architecture and validation lessons]\nrollout_summaries/2026-09-26T17-48-16-Ok1N-xauusd_local_ai_research_handbook_pdf.md:23-28|note=[separation of research learning and numerical evaluation]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 7, "text_sha256": "8520d27aa11cc9884bb2e3c30ad5e5785499f39c2981c2649af75e3742ed3230", "section": "conversation"}
