{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 13, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:15:36.339Z", "message_id": "01a13ea1-896a-7911-8424-4ea404d2dc6f", "role": "user", "phase": null, "parts": ["now i will make app enhanced, there will be 8 extinction, smc ,liquidity swipt ,volume profile ,fvg, ict, ema, supply demand,prize action. its a very big project . so we will go step by step, now will will start with smc, give me a short summery of how smc works? what are the propertices and application?\n"], "text": "now i will make app enhanced, there will be 8 extinction, smc ,liquidity swipt ,volume profile ,fvg, ict, ema, supply demand,prize action. its a very big project . so we will go step by step, now will will start with smc, give me a short summery of how smc works? what are the propertices and application?\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 58, "text_sha256": "c51e146bdb1691301d38a7da4128b7917ab4a77e6c355762a9b204c6e4faefdc", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 16, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:15:58.145Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08bbdc38c8191883d5a001bd6f960", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Iâ€™ll check the main SMC concepts and explain how they could become features in your app.\n\n"], "text": "Iâ€™ll check the main SMC concepts and explain how they could become features in your app.\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 59, "text_sha256": "d8a5ca7c0bfbdb8932db4db16717823c2596ad4b110249b2e6b1c872181225d6", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 43, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:16:53.588Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08be694d0819184688cd2cd11fc3b", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**SMC means Smart Money Concepts.** It is a way of reading price charts through trends, important price zones, and possible liquidity locations. Traders use these patterns to interpret large participantsâ€™ activity, but a chart pattern cannot prove who traded or whether manipulation occurred. [SMC overview](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\nIts main components are:\n\n| Component | Meaning | Application |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Market structure** | Higher highs/higher lows suggest an uptrend; lower highs/lower lows suggest a downtrend. | Establish market direction. |\n| **BOS â€” Break of Structure** | Price breaks a significant swing in the existing trendâ€™s direction. | Identify possible continuation. |\n| **CHoCH â€” Change of Character** | Price breaks important structure against the existing trend. | Flag a possible reversal; it needs further confirmation. |\n| **Order blocks** | Candidate supply/demand zones associated with a strong move, often marked around an opposing candle before it. | Identify areas to watch for a reaction. |\n| **Liquidity and sweeps** | Prior highs/lows are treated as possible stop-order clusters; a sweep moves beyond a level and returns inside. | Investigate failed breakouts and potential targets. |\n| **FVG â€” Fair Value Gap** | A three-candle price imbalance where the first and third candlesâ€™ ranges do not overlap. | Mark possible retracement zones; a return is not guaranteed. |\n| **Premium/discount** | Priceâ€™s position above/below the midpoint of a selected range. | Judge entry location relative to that range. |\n\nThese concepts form a chart-reading framework rather than one fixed strategy. [Structure definitions](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/), [SMC components](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/the-smart-money-concept-basics-and-strategies/)\n\nA typical approach is: **identify the trend â†’ mark relevant zones â†’ wait for price to reach one â†’ require a defined confirmation â†’ set entry, invalidation, and target.** Its effectiveness must be measured with explicit rules and realistic trading costs.\n\nFor **MarketLinkâ€™s first SMC extension**, I recommend starting with:\n\n- Confirmed swing highs/lows and bullish/bearish/ranging state.\n- BOS and CHoCH chart labels and alerts.\n- Adjustable swing sensitivity and candle-close versus wick-break rules.\n- Historical replay that records when each signal actually became available.\n\nYour eight extensions overlapâ€”SMC already includes several liquidity, FVG, and supply/demand ideas. We should share their underlying calculations so the modules remain consistent."], "text": "**SMC means Smart Money Concepts.** It is a way of reading price charts through trends, important price zones, and possible liquidity locations. Traders use these patterns to interpret large participantsâ€™ activity, but a chart pattern cannot prove who traded or whether manipulation occurred. [SMC overview](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\nIts main components are:\n\n| Component | Meaning | Application |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Market structure** | Higher highs/higher lows suggest an uptrend; lower highs/lower lows suggest a downtrend. | Establish market direction. |\n| **BOS â€” Break of Structure** | Price breaks a significant swing in the existing trendâ€™s direction. | Identify possible continuation. |\n| **CHoCH â€” Change of Character** | Price breaks important structure against the existing trend. | Flag a possible reversal; it needs further confirmation. |\n| **Order blocks** | Candidate supply/demand zones associated with a strong move, often marked around an opposing candle before it. | Identify areas to watch for a reaction. |\n| **Liquidity and sweeps** | Prior highs/lows are treated as possible stop-order clusters; a sweep moves beyond a level and returns inside. | Investigate failed breakouts and potential targets. |\n| **FVG â€” Fair Value Gap** | A three-candle price imbalance where the first and third candlesâ€™ ranges do not overlap. | Mark possible retracement zones; a return is not guaranteed. |\n| **Premium/discount** | Priceâ€™s position above/below the midpoint of a selected range. | Judge entry location relative to that range. |\n\nThese concepts form a chart-reading framework rather than one fixed strategy. [Structure definitions](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/), [SMC components](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/the-smart-money-concept-basics-and-strategies/)\n\nA typical approach is: **identify the trend â†’ mark relevant zones â†’ wait for price to reach one â†’ require a defined confirmation â†’ set entry, invalidation, and target.** Its effectiveness must be measured with explicit rules and realistic trading costs.\n\nFor **MarketLinkâ€™s first SMC extension**, I recommend starting with:\n\n- Confirmed swing highs/lows and bullish/bearish/ranging state.\n- BOS and CHoCH chart labels and alerts.\n- Adjustable swing sensitivity and candle-close versus wick-break rules.\n- Historical replay that records when each signal actually became available.\n\nYour eight extensions overlapâ€”SMC already includes several liquidity, FVG, and supply/demand ideas. We should share their underlying calculations so the modules remain consistent.", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 60, "text_sha256": "e6ca653e0e609f9075b26e6f394ed6ea870a26e2d2d5424d1674f31cdfce55d2", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 52, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:18:23.383Z", "message_id": "01a13ea4-1602-71c0-9800-a9cd72da6f01", "role": "user", "phase": null, "parts": ["when we should buy sell or wait according to this strategy (start deep research) and anwer\n"], "text": "when we should buy sell or wait according to this strategy (start deep research) and anwer\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 61, "text_sha256": "b547cb4c20fadf16576c53522622ba6bee1964a8f36b3caa26f49f038e001fa5", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 53, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:18:27.226Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08c5205e881918207fd60c51b97fd", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Iâ€™ll research how SMC traders define Buy, Sell, and Wait decisions, then turn that into explicit rules we can test in MarketLink. Iâ€™ll distinguish common teaching from evidence that a rule is profitable, and flag definitions that vary between traders.\n\n"], "text": "Iâ€™ll research how SMC traders define Buy, Sell, and Wait decisions, then turn that into explicit rules we can test in MarketLink. Iâ€™ll distinguish common teaching from evidence that a rule is profitable, and flag definitions that vary between traders.\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 62, "text_sha256": "41b3a20db196dc5d13afc35a3e02acb5687f29e83890424ab77dbea565169400", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 99, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:19:55.485Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08caa03dc8191907aefe79e2ca800", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["One important finding: a move through an old high or low can accelerate the trend as well as reverse it. For our first testable SMC model, a â€œliquidity sweepâ€ alone should therefore leave the app at Wait; a reversal setup needs a reclaim, a structure shift, and an entry/risk check.\n\n"], "text": "One important finding: a move through an old high or low can accelerate the trend as well as reverse it. For our first testable SMC model, a â€œliquidity sweepâ€ alone should therefore leave the app at Wait; a reversal setup needs a reclaim, a structure shift, and an entry/risk check.\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 63, "text_sha256": "0039b8c8845af3ecd2b7dadd830135f25f51e82789b5a2bb9910140c2919ba2c", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 132, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:22:37.793Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08d1318508191b3f71b11f28cc1a8", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**Buy when bullish context, a completed bullish trigger, and acceptable trade economics agree. Sell when the bearish equivalents agree. Otherwise, Wait.**\n\nThe research supports turning SMC into a **sequence of conditions**. An order block, FVG, sweep, or CHoCH by itself does not establish an entry.\n\nSMC also has no single standardized strategy. Below I distinguish common trading practice from **a proposed rule set for our app**, whose profitability still needs testing.\n\n**What the research actually supports**\n\nResearch from the New York Fed found that currency stop-loss and take-profit orders can cluster around certain levels. Crucially, triggering stops can **accelerate an existing trend**. Therefore, â€œprice crossed yesterdayâ€™s high, so sellâ€ is unreliable reasoning: the move might continue. These historical FX findings do not directly validate an SMC strategy on Exness gold. [Order clustering research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr125.html), [stop-loss cascades](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\nPractitioner sources describe a common reversal sequence: **sweep a level â†’ reclaim it â†’ break structure in the opposite direction â†’ enter using a defined confirmation or retracement rule**. This is a trading hypothesis, not proof of a profitable edge. [FXOpenâ€™s explanation](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/)\n\nCandles cannot establish which institution traded, whether hidden orders remain, or whether someone deliberately manipulated price. We should label these as **inferred liquidity levels and candidate reaction zones**. [Order-block definitions and limitations](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When would we consider a Buy?**\n\nOne coherent SMC approach is to look for the end of a small downward pullback within a broader upward trend.\n\n| Check | What we need to observe | Decision before it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Broader direction** | The higher timeframe has confirmed higher highs and higher lows. | Wait if direction is unclear. |\n| **2. Pullback location** | Price returns to a predefined area of interest, rather than entering after a large upward move. | Wait for price to arrive. |\n| **3. Downside sweep and reclaim** | Price trades below an already identified local low, then closes back above it. | A break below alone means Wait. |\n| **4. Bullish structure shift** | A later completed candle closes above a relevant, previously known local swing high. | A wick through that high is insufficient under a close-based rule. |\n| **5. Entry condition** | The selected entry model completes: confirmation entry **or** a specified retracement/rejection entry. | Do not switch models halfway through. |\n| **6. Stop and target** | A defensible stop and a target identified before entry leave sufficient potential reward after costs. | Wait or reject if they do not. |\n\nThe higher timeframe can remain bullish while the lower-timeframe pullback is bearish. A bullish CHoCH on the lower timeframe can therefore signal the **possible end of that pullback**, without implying a brand-new higher-timeframe uptrend. BOS and CHoCH must always be tied to the structure being measured. [Structure concepts](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\n**When would we consider a Sell?**\n\nUse the exact opposite sequence:\n\n1. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish.\n2. Price rallies into a predefined area of interest.\n3. It moves above an already identified local high and closes back below.\n4. A later completed candle closes below the relevant local swing low.\n5. The chosen entry condition completes.\n6. A stop above the invalidation area and a predefined downside target leave sufficient potential reward after costs.\n\n**A bullish market does not become a Sell simply because price looks expensive.** Likewise, a bearish market does not become a Buy simply because price has fallen substantially.\n\n**Continuation and reversal need separate rules**\n\nTwo common SMC approaches should be evaluated separately:\n\n| Model | Typical sequence |\n|---|---|\n| **Trend continuation** | Established trend â†’ BOS with that trend â†’ retracement into the breaking moveâ€™s candidate order block â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n| **Sweep and structure shift** | Cross a known high/low â†’ reclaim it â†’ opposite structure break â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n\nA sweep-and-shift setup can occur inside a higher-timeframe pullback or against the broader trend. Those are different experiments; we should record them separately.\n\nThere are also two different entry methods:\n\n- **Confirmation entry:** enter after the required reaction or structure break completes. It enters later and may leave less reward relative to the stop.\n- **Retracement entry:** wait for price to revisit a predetermined level or zone. It may offer a better entry price, but can remain unfilled or enter while the zone fails.\n\nNeither method is universally superior. They require separate tests using the same original setup definitions. [Entry-method trade-offs](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When should the app say Wait?**\n\nWait should be an explicit, explainable result.\n\n| Situation | App explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Structure is mixed or ranging | **WAIT â€” no directional context** |\n| Price only touches an order block/FVG | **WAIT â€” entry trigger incomplete** |\n| Price crosses a high/low but does not reclaim it | **WAIT â€” sweep/rejection unconfirmed** |\n| Reclaim occurs without the required structure break | **WAIT â€” structure confirmation missing** |\n| The model requires a retracement and none occurs | **WAIT â€” awaiting retracement** |\n| Price has already moved close to the target | **REJECT â€” insufficient remaining reward** |\n| The zone or setupâ€™s invalidation boundary is breached | **CANCEL â€” setup invalidated** |\n| Confirmation takes too long | **CANCEL â€” setup expired** |\n| Spread, quotes, market permissions, or data fail checks | **BLOCK â€” execution conditions unsuitable** |\n| Position or exposure limits are reached | **BLOCK â€” risk limit reached** |\n\nA news exclusion window can also be tested, but its timing must be defined in advance rather than added after observing losing trades.\n\n**My proposed first experiment for MarketLink**\n\nTo start with manageable rules, I would test the following **demo research model**. These settings are design choices, not proven optimal values.\n\n| Element | Initial experimental definition |\n|---|---|\n| Context timeframe | H1 |\n| Trigger timeframe | M5 |\n| Swing detection | A pivot with two completed candles on each side |\n| Bullish/bearish context | Latest two confirmed highs **and** lows both rising/falling; otherwise Wait |\n| Sweep | Cross a local level known before the candle began, then close back inside |\n| Confirmation | A subsequent candle closes beyond the opposite pre-sweep swing |\n| Confirmation expiry | Six M5 candles |\n| Entry baseline | First tradable quote after confirmation completes |\n| Stop | Beyond the sweep extreme, with a predefined buffer |\n| Target | A relevant opposing swing selected and frozen before entry |\n| Reward/risk gate | Test a minimum **2:1 after estimated costs** |\n| Position handling | One position; fixed initial SL/TP; one attempt per setup |\n\nFor this baseline, order-block and FVG retracement entries would be **separate later variants**. That lets us measure whether each addition improves results.\n\nSwing timing matters: a two-right-candle pivot becomes known only after those later candles close. A historical label placed on the pivot candle must not allow the backtest to enter there earlier. [MetaQuotesâ€™ five-bar fractal definition](https://www.mql5.com/en/code/30)\n\n**Why entry price can change Buy into Wait**\n\nConsider hypothetical prices, before costs:\n\n- Entry **100**, stop **98**, target **105**: potential reward/risk is **5 Ã· 2 = 2.5:1**.\n- If entry is delayed until **104**, with the same stop and target: **1 Ã· 6 â‰ˆ 0.17:1**.\n\nThe direction might still be bullish, but the second trade fails the proposed entry gate. We should not move the target farther away merely to manufacture an attractive ratio.\n\nA 2:1 target also does not guarantee profitability; the realized win rate, losses, slippage, and costs determine expectancy.\n\n**What matters specifically for your MT5 bridge**\n\nExness documents that a Buy opens at **Ask** and closes at **Bid**, while a Sell opens at **Bid** and closes at **Ask**. Backtests must use those executable prices, spread, commission, and slippage. A chart-only touch is insufficient to assume a fill. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\nThe app should eventually display the **current decision, completed conditions, missing condition, invalidation level, target, and signal time**. We should avoid displaying an invented â€œ90% confidenceâ€ score.\n\nBefore enabling strategy-driven orders, freeze the rules, evaluate them chronologically on unseen data, record every attempted variant, and forward-test on demo. Repeatedly choosing the best-looking backtest can produce a convincing strategy with no durable edge. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\n"], "text": "**Buy when bullish context, a completed bullish trigger, and acceptable trade economics agree. Sell when the bearish equivalents agree. Otherwise, Wait.**\n\nThe research supports turning SMC into a **sequence of conditions**. An order block, FVG, sweep, or CHoCH by itself does not establish an entry.\n\nSMC also has no single standardized strategy. Below I distinguish common trading practice from **a proposed rule set for our app**, whose profitability still needs testing.\n\n**What the research actually supports**\n\nResearch from the New York Fed found that currency stop-loss and take-profit orders can cluster around certain levels. Crucially, triggering stops can **accelerate an existing trend**. Therefore, â€œprice crossed yesterdayâ€™s high, so sellâ€ is unreliable reasoning: the move might continue. These historical FX findings do not directly validate an SMC strategy on Exness gold. [Order clustering research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr125.html), [stop-loss cascades](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\nPractitioner sources describe a common reversal sequence: **sweep a level â†’ reclaim it â†’ break structure in the opposite direction â†’ enter using a defined confirmation or retracement rule**. This is a trading hypothesis, not proof of a profitable edge. [FXOpenâ€™s explanation](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/)\n\nCandles cannot establish which institution traded, whether hidden orders remain, or whether someone deliberately manipulated price. We should label these as **inferred liquidity levels and candidate reaction zones**. [Order-block definitions and limitations](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When would we consider a Buy?**\n\nOne coherent SMC approach is to look for the end of a small downward pullback within a broader upward trend.\n\n| Check | What we need to observe | Decision before it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Broader direction** | The higher timeframe has confirmed higher highs and higher lows. | Wait if direction is unclear. |\n| **2. Pullback location** | Price returns to a predefined area of interest, rather than entering after a large upward move. | Wait for price to arrive. |\n| **3. Downside sweep and reclaim** | Price trades below an already identified local low, then closes back above it. | A break below alone means Wait. |\n| **4. Bullish structure shift** | A later completed candle closes above a relevant, previously known local swing high. | A wick through that high is insufficient under a close-based rule. |\n| **5. Entry condition** | The selected entry model completes: confirmation entry **or** a specified retracement/rejection entry. | Do not switch models halfway through. |\n| **6. Stop and target** | A defensible stop and a target identified before entry leave sufficient potential reward after costs. | Wait or reject if they do not. |\n\nThe higher timeframe can remain bullish while the lower-timeframe pullback is bearish. A bullish CHoCH on the lower timeframe can therefore signal the **possible end of that pullback**, without implying a brand-new higher-timeframe uptrend. BOS and CHoCH must always be tied to the structure being measured. [Structure concepts](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\n**When would we consider a Sell?**\n\nUse the exact opposite sequence:\n\n1. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish.\n2. Price rallies into a predefined area of interest.\n3. It moves above an already identified local high and closes back below.\n4. A later completed candle closes below the relevant local swing low.\n5. The chosen entry condition completes.\n6. A stop above the invalidation area and a predefined downside target leave sufficient potential reward after costs.\n\n**A bullish market does not become a Sell simply because price looks expensive.** Likewise, a bearish market does not become a Buy simply because price has fallen substantially.\n\n**Continuation and reversal need separate rules**\n\nTwo common SMC approaches should be evaluated separately:\n\n| Model | Typical sequence |\n|---|---|\n| **Trend continuation** | Established trend â†’ BOS with that trend â†’ retracement into the breaking moveâ€™s candidate order block â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n| **Sweep and structure shift** | Cross a known high/low â†’ reclaim it â†’ opposite structure break â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n\nA sweep-and-shift setup can occur inside a higher-timeframe pullback or against the broader trend. Those are different experiments; we should record them separately.\n\nThere are also two different entry methods:\n\n- **Confirmation entry:** enter after the required reaction or structure break completes. It enters later and may leave less reward relative to the stop.\n- **Retracement entry:** wait for price to revisit a predetermined level or zone. It may offer a better entry price, but can remain unfilled or enter while the zone fails.\n\nNeither method is universally superior. They require separate tests using the same original setup definitions. [Entry-method trade-offs](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When should the app say Wait?**\n\nWait should be an explicit, explainable result.\n\n| Situation | App explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Structure is mixed or ranging | **WAIT â€” no directional context** |\n| Price only touches an order block/FVG | **WAIT â€” entry trigger incomplete** |\n| Price crosses a high/low but does not reclaim it | **WAIT â€” sweep/rejection unconfirmed** |\n| Reclaim occurs without the required structure break | **WAIT â€” structure confirmation missing** |\n| The model requires a retracement and none occurs | **WAIT â€” awaiting retracement** |\n| Price has already moved close to the target | **REJECT â€” insufficient remaining reward** |\n| The zone or setupâ€™s invalidation boundary is breached | **CANCEL â€” setup invalidated** |\n| Confirmation takes too long | **CANCEL â€” setup expired** |\n| Spread, quotes, market permissions, or data fail checks | **BLOCK â€” execution conditions unsuitable** |\n| Position or exposure limits are reached | **BLOCK â€” risk limit reached** |\n\nA news exclusion window can also be tested, but its timing must be defined in advance rather than added after observing losing trades.\n\n**My proposed first experiment for MarketLink**\n\nTo start with manageable rules, I would test the following **demo research model**. These settings are design choices, not proven optimal values.\n\n| Element | Initial experimental definition |\n|---|---|\n| Context timeframe | H1 |\n| Trigger timeframe | M5 |\n| Swing detection | A pivot with two completed candles on each side |\n| Bullish/bearish context | Latest two confirmed highs **and** lows both rising/falling; otherwise Wait |\n| Sweep | Cross a local level known before the candle began, then close back inside |\n| Confirmation | A subsequent candle closes beyond the opposite pre-sweep swing |\n| Confirmation expiry | Six M5 candles |\n| Entry baseline | First tradable quote after confirmation completes |\n| Stop | Beyond the sweep extreme, with a predefined buffer |\n| Target | A relevant opposing swing selected and frozen before entry |\n| Reward/risk gate | Test a minimum **2:1 after estimated costs** |\n| Position handling | One position; fixed initial SL/TP; one attempt per setup |\n\nFor this baseline, order-block and FVG retracement entries would be **separate later variants**. That lets us measure whether each addition improves results.\n\nSwing timing matters: a two-right-candle pivot becomes known only after those later candles close. A historical label placed on the pivot candle must not allow the backtest to enter there earlier. [MetaQuotesâ€™ five-bar fractal definition](https://www.mql5.com/en/code/30)\n\n**Why entry price can change Buy into Wait**\n\nConsider hypothetical prices, before costs:\n\n- Entry **100**, stop **98**, target **105**: potential reward/risk is **5 Ã· 2 = 2.5:1**.\n- If entry is delayed until **104**, with the same stop and target: **1 Ã· 6 â‰ˆ 0.17:1**.\n\nThe direction might still be bullish, but the second trade fails the proposed entry gate. We should not move the target farther away merely to manufacture an attractive ratio.\n\nA 2:1 target also does not guarantee profitability; the realized win rate, losses, slippage, and costs determine expectancy.\n\n**What matters specifically for your MT5 bridge**\n\nExness documents that a Buy opens at **Ask** and closes at **Bid**, while a Sell opens at **Bid** and closes at **Ask**. Backtests must use those executable prices, spread, commission, and slippage. A chart-only touch is insufficient to assume a fill. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\nThe app should eventually display the **current decision, completed conditions, missing condition, invalidation level, target, and signal time**. We should avoid displaying an invented â€œ90% confidenceâ€ score.\n\nBefore enabling strategy-driven orders, freeze the rules, evaluate them chronologically on unseen data, record every attempted variant, and forward-test on demo. Repeatedly choosing the best-looking backtest can produce a convincing strategy with no durable edge. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 64, "text_sha256": "4d5ab016fe6b824a95b3679e00f59df567a85d89c3121110ad9a3b332458224a", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 151, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T09:00:31.499Z", "message_id": "01a13eca-a975-7cb0-ab4e-0f83354d0927", "role": "user", "phase": null, "parts": ["whats are the variables hare , by backtesting in the merket and changing value can give extreme profit?\n"], "text": "whats are the variables hare , by backtesting in the merket and changing value can give extreme profit?\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 65, "text_sha256": "4dc9a03fc89704b581ede71f2333409cb87df130e00fae60ac248b855fc462ca", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 152, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T09:00:35.203Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad096322c0c8191b90db2e6e0bad530", "role": "assistant", "phase": "commentary", "parts": ["Iâ€™ll map the SMC rules into variables we can test, and explain how to tell a real improvement from an impressive-looking backtest.\n\n"], "text": "Iâ€™ll map the SMC rules into variables we can test, and explain how to tell a real improvement from an impressive-looking backtest.\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 66, "text_sha256": "27921a93627cfcd6ceaffc4a6be3c2e8c2aefae6e1470199bec62900eea325b8", "section": "conversation"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 176, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T09:02:00.218Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad09670556c8191a6a5d277c7176bd2", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**Yesâ€”changing the variables can improve a strategyâ€™s results. But it can also produce â€œextreme profitâ€ on historical data that disappears in live trading.** The task is to find settings that continue working on unseen data, after realistic costs.\n\nFor our SMC model, these are the main adjustable variables. **The example values below are research choices, not proven profitable settings.**\n\n| Variable | What it controls | Example values or choices |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Context timeframe** | Which chart defines the broader direction | M15, H1, H4 |\n| **Entry timeframe** | Which chart supplies the trigger | M1, M3, M5, M15 |\n| **Swing sensitivity** | How many candles on each side confirm a high/low | 2, 3, or 5 per side |\n| **Sweep distance** | How far price must cross a known level | A fixed number of points or a fraction of volatility |\n| **Reclaim rule** | What confirms price returned inside the level | Same-candle close or a later close |\n| **Structure-break rule** | What qualifies as BOS/CHoCH | Wick crossing versus completed candle close |\n| **Displacement threshold** | How strong the confirmation candle must be | Body â‰¥ 0.6, 0.8, or 1.0 Ã— ATR |\n| **Setup expiry** | How long confirmation remains eligible | 3, 6, or 12 candles after the sweep |\n| **Entry method** | When to enter after confirmation | Next available quote, order-block retest, or FVG retest |\n| **Zone definition** | How an order block or FVG is constructed | Full candle range, candle body, minimum gap size |\n| **Stop buffer** | Extra distance beyond the invalidation level | 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n| **Target rule** | Where to take profit | A structural swing or a fixed reward multiple |\n| **Trade filters** | Conditions that permit or block an entry | Session, spread, volatility, news window |\n| **Exit management** | What happens after entry | Fixed SL/TP, trailing stop, break-even, time exit |\n\n**ATR measures recent price volatility.** Any ATR-based condition should use information available before the decision. Definitions, confirmation delays, entries, and exits must remain consistent throughout a test. [Order-block research guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**Some variables affect exposure rather than the quality of the signal.**\n\nLot size, leverage, and risk per trade can dramatically increase historical profit **and losses**. Increasing them does not make the entry prediction more accurate. We should compare strategy settings using consistent position sizing.\n\nSpread, commission, and slippage are execution assumptions. We must model them realistically. Exness buys execute at Ask and sells at Bid; using one candle price for everything can distort results. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\n**For the first experiment, I would change only four variables.**\n\nKeep H1 context, M5 triggers, completed-candle rules, ATR period 14, entry method, target rule, and position sizing fixed.\n\n| Variable | Three experimental values |\n|---|---|\n| Swing confirmation length | 2 / 3 / 5 candles per side |\n| Confirmation body Ã· prior completed M5 ATR | 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 |\n| Confirmation expiry after sweep | 3 / 6 / 12 M5 candles |\n| Stop buffer beyond sweep extreme | 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n\nThat gives **3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 = 81 combinations**. We should also compare the displacement filter against a baseline without it.\n\n**How we decide whether an improvement is credible:**\n\n1. Develop settings on the earliest data.\n2. Compare candidates on later validation data.\n3. Freeze the selected rules before evaluating an untouched final period.\n4. Record every attempted configuration, including failures.\n5. Check nearby settings: a broad region of acceptable results is more reassuring than one exceptional combination.\n6. Forward-test the frozen version on demo.\n\nRepeatedly searching the same history increases the chance of selecting noise. Even a holdout becomes compromised if we keep using its results to redesign the strategy. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\nWe should measure **net expectancy, profit factor, drawdown, trade count, exposure, and stability across periods**. No parameter combination can be called profitable for our app yetâ€”we have verified the trading bridge, but have not backtested this SMC strategy.\n\n"], "text": "**Yesâ€”changing the variables can improve a strategyâ€™s results. But it can also produce â€œextreme profitâ€ on historical data that disappears in live trading.** The task is to find settings that continue working on unseen data, after realistic costs.\n\nFor our SMC model, these are the main adjustable variables. **The example values below are research choices, not proven profitable settings.**\n\n| Variable | What it controls | Example values or choices |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Context timeframe** | Which chart defines the broader direction | M15, H1, H4 |\n| **Entry timeframe** | Which chart supplies the trigger | M1, M3, M5, M15 |\n| **Swing sensitivity** | How many candles on each side confirm a high/low | 2, 3, or 5 per side |\n| **Sweep distance** | How far price must cross a known level | A fixed number of points or a fraction of volatility |\n| **Reclaim rule** | What confirms price returned inside the level | Same-candle close or a later close |\n| **Structure-break rule** | What qualifies as BOS/CHoCH | Wick crossing versus completed candle close |\n| **Displacement threshold** | How strong the confirmation candle must be | Body â‰¥ 0.6, 0.8, or 1.0 Ã— ATR |\n| **Setup expiry** | How long confirmation remains eligible | 3, 6, or 12 candles after the sweep |\n| **Entry method** | When to enter after confirmation | Next available quote, order-block retest, or FVG retest |\n| **Zone definition** | How an order block or FVG is constructed | Full candle range, candle body, minimum gap size |\n| **Stop buffer** | Extra distance beyond the invalidation level | 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n| **Target rule** | Where to take profit | A structural swing or a fixed reward multiple |\n| **Trade filters** | Conditions that permit or block an entry | Session, spread, volatility, news window |\n| **Exit management** | What happens after entry | Fixed SL/TP, trailing stop, break-even, time exit |\n\n**ATR measures recent price volatility.** Any ATR-based condition should use information available before the decision. Definitions, confirmation delays, entries, and exits must remain consistent throughout a test. [Order-block research guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**Some variables affect exposure rather than the quality of the signal.**\n\nLot size, leverage, and risk per trade can dramatically increase historical profit **and losses**. Increasing them does not make the entry prediction more accurate. We should compare strategy settings using consistent position sizing.\n\nSpread, commission, and slippage are execution assumptions. We must model them realistically. Exness buys execute at Ask and sells at Bid; using one candle price for everything can distort results. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\n**For the first experiment, I would change only four variables.**\n\nKeep H1 context, M5 triggers, completed-candle rules, ATR period 14, entry method, target rule, and position sizing fixed.\n\n| Variable | Three experimental values |\n|---|---|\n| Swing confirmation length | 2 / 3 / 5 candles per side |\n| Confirmation body Ã· prior completed M5 ATR | 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 |\n| Confirmation expiry after sweep | 3 / 6 / 12 M5 candles |\n| Stop buffer beyond sweep extreme | 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n\nThat gives **3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 = 81 combinations**. We should also compare the displacement filter against a baseline without it.\n\n**How we decide whether an improvement is credible:**\n\n1. Develop settings on the earliest data.\n2. Compare candidates on later validation data.\n3. Freeze the selected rules before evaluating an untouched final period.\n4. Record every attempted configuration, including failures.\n5. Check nearby settings: a broad region of acceptable results is more reassuring than one exceptional combination.\n6. Forward-test the frozen version on demo.\n\nRepeatedly searching the same history increases the chance of selecting noise. Even a holdout becomes compromised if we keep using its results to redesign the strategy. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\nWe should measure **net expectancy, profit factor, drawdown, trade count, exposure, and stability across periods**. No parameter combination can be called profitable for our app yetâ€”we have verified the trading bridge, but have not backtested this SMC strategy.\n\n", "images": [], "source_kind": "visible_chat_event", "index": 67, "text_sha256": "06677c37502185ddd466877061aaa6fd09796605274cdf7d90d8580fab14e431", "section": "conversation"}
