{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 1260, "timestamp": "2026-10-13T19:38:54.612Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ace88c7ea10819188dfbd3c8e45214e", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["Created the **49-page PDF** with exactly **20,000 words of AI instructions across 40 build modules**, plus navigation and references.\n\nIt includes the master prompt, implementation steps, tests, acceptance gates, and handoff instructions. Every page, link destination, and module word count was checked.\n\nUpload it to your AI coding assistant and start with **Module 01 on page 7**.\n\n:codex-file-citation{path=\"C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/output/pdf/Market_Learning_AI_20000_Word_Build_Prompt.pdf\" purpose=\"output\"}\n\n[Plain-text copy for easy pasting](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/output/pdf/Market_Learning_AI_20000_Word_Build_Prompt.txt>)\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-234|note=[separation of AI research and numerical backtesting]\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[data provenance and untouched evaluation]\nMEMORY.md:239-239|note=[document verification requirements]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "Created the **49-page PDF** with exactly **20,000 words of AI instructions across 40 build modules**, plus navigation and references.\n\nIt includes the master prompt, implementation steps, tests, acceptance gates, and handoff instructions. Every page, link destination, and module word count was checked.\n\nUpload it to your AI coding assistant and start with **Module 01 on page 7**.\n\n:codex-file-citation{path=\"C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/output/pdf/Market_Learning_AI_20000_Word_Build_Prompt.pdf\" purpose=\"output\"}\n\n[Plain-text copy for easy pasting](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/output/pdf/Market_Learning_AI_20000_Word_Build_Prompt.txt>)\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-234|note=[separation of AI research and numerical backtesting]\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[data provenance and untouched evaluation]\nMEMORY.md:239-239|note=[document verification requirements]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 3, "text_sha256": "55ef8667df6c75338795cea982b18f27a14c3777dd5808579a7fc5e6486a1bd8", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 1489, "timestamp": "2026-10-13T21:16:15.622Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ace9f965e9881918884ab843887292b", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["Checked it. **Itâ€™s a useful offline starter, but four correctness issues need fixing:**\n\n- **Margin checks:** reversing a position can open new exposure without enough margin.\n- **Data timing:** receipt-based records can expose information before it actually arrived, allowing future-data leakage.\n- **Report handling:** one malformed manifest can crash the entire review index.\n- **MT5 intervals:** fractional-millisecond boundaries can select the wrong ticks.\n\nI reproduced these with offline probes.\n\nOn Windows with its pinned Python 3.13 dependencies, **112 tests passed, 1 failed, and 2 were skipped**. The failure concerns Windows path normalization. The default synthetic results and checkpoint recovery reproduced correctly.\n\n**It does not yet learn strategies or backtest your actual Exness history.** Tick export, storage, model training, agents, and memory remain unfinishedâ€”and the documentation honestly acknowledges that.\n\n[Full review, evidence, and recommended fixes](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/reviews/market-learning-starter-20261014-e41d09f/REVIEW.md>)\n\nYour ZIP was preserved; I made no source fixes or MT5 connections.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-234|note=[separate AI research from numerical backtesting]\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[data provenance and evaluation review criteria]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "Checked it. **Itâ€™s a useful offline starter, but four correctness issues need fixing:**\n\n- **Margin checks:** reversing a position can open new exposure without enough margin.\n- **Data timing:** receipt-based records can expose information before it actually arrived, allowing future-data leakage.\n- **Report handling:** one malformed manifest can crash the entire review index.\n- **MT5 intervals:** fractional-millisecond boundaries can select the wrong ticks.\n\nI reproduced these with offline probes.\n\nOn Windows with its pinned Python 3.13 dependencies, **112 tests passed, 1 failed, and 2 were skipped**. The failure concerns Windows path normalization. The default synthetic results and checkpoint recovery reproduced correctly.\n\n**It does not yet learn strategies or backtest your actual Exness history.** Tick export, storage, model training, agents, and memory remain unfinishedâ€”and the documentation honestly acknowledges that.\n\n[Full review, evidence, and recommended fixes](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Desktop/codex/reviews/market-learning-starter-20261014-e41d09f/REVIEW.md>)\n\nYour ZIP was preserved; I made no source fixes or MT5 connections.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:234-234|note=[separate AI research from numerical backtesting]\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[data provenance and evaluation review criteria]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 4, "text_sha256": "0cc265eb1cfcd0a6e94b899e98dbff65a3979bdb7ea7ecf4fc60043446a720b3", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 2296, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T04:08:34.650Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad051bacbb08191b7d082274a892dd2", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**Done. MarketLink is installed and open.** Use the **MarketLink** desktop shortcut. The EA is installed under **MT5 â†’ Expert Advisors â†’ MarketLink** and attached to XAUUSDm.\n\nVerified on your Exness **demo account**: Buy, Sell, initial SL/TP, SL/TP modification, and Close. All **27 checks passed**; the EA compiled with **0 errors and 0 warnings**.\n\n- [Windows installer](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLink-Setup.exe>)\n- [Complete package with source code](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0.zip>)\n- EA: [MQ5 source](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLinkBridge.mq5>) Â· [Compiled EX5](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLinkBridge.ex5>)\n- [Instructions](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/README.txt>) Â· [Test report](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/TEST-REPORT.md>)\n\nMy test positions are closed. **Your earlier demo Sell remains open.** Real-money execution has not been tested.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:145-145|note=[Prior MT5 environment context rechecked against the local terminal]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "**Done. MarketLink is installed and open.** Use the **MarketLink** desktop shortcut. The EA is installed under **MT5 â†’ Expert Advisors â†’ MarketLink** and attached to XAUUSDm.\n\nVerified on your Exness **demo account**: Buy, Sell, initial SL/TP, SL/TP modification, and Close. All **27 checks passed**; the EA compiled with **0 errors and 0 warnings**.\n\n- [Windows installer](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLink-Setup.exe>)\n- [Complete package with source code](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0.zip>)\n- EA: [MQ5 source](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLinkBridge.mq5>) Â· [Compiled EX5](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/MarketLinkBridge.ex5>)\n- [Instructions](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/README.txt>) Â· [Test report](<C:/Users/EHOSANUR RAHMAN ROMI/Downloads/MarketLink-1.0.0/TEST-REPORT.md>)\n\nMy test positions are closed. **Your earlier demo Sell remains open.** Real-money execution has not been tested.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:145-145|note=[Prior MT5 environment context rechecked against the local terminal]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 5, "text_sha256": "7993eea0869ec0fa044367bca2c3adc8eff0d9f561ee91a3600d2fc5fa9e05e0", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-13T01-21-24-01a13190-04da-7773-af76-0aa89a528609.jsonl", "line": 2411, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T04:21:58.422Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad054e2ddcc8191afca99743233f9b7", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["Created your private repository: [marketlink-mt5-bridge](https://github.com/EhosanurRahmanRomi/marketlink-mt5-bridge).\n\nUploaded **18 files**: app and EA source, installer, build scripts, tests, and documentation. Verified every uploaded file against the local commit.\n\nAccount details, trading logs, and local runtime data remain on your PC.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:106-106|note=[Applied prior credential-handling guidance to the upload selection]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0f138-7802-7fd1-a01c-2efb65a6cad6\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "Created your private repository: [marketlink-mt5-bridge](https://github.com/EhosanurRahmanRomi/marketlink-mt5-bridge).\n\nUploaded **18 files**: app and EA source, installer, build scripts, tests, and documentation. Verified every uploaded file against the local commit.\n\nAccount details, trading logs, and local runtime data remain on your PC.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:106-106|note=[Applied prior credential-handling guidance to the upload selection]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0f138-7802-7fd1-a01c-2efb65a6cad6\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 6, "text_sha256": "4d8d77b4bcf57bfa4ef84e67add876d43df85d690de975c61c9c3667a2ca8803", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 133, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T08:22:37.819Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad08d1318508191b3f71b11f28cc1a8", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**Buy when bullish context, a completed bullish trigger, and acceptable trade economics agree. Sell when the bearish equivalents agree. Otherwise, Wait.**\n\nThe research supports turning SMC into a **sequence of conditions**. An order block, FVG, sweep, or CHoCH by itself does not establish an entry.\n\nSMC also has no single standardized strategy. Below I distinguish common trading practice from **a proposed rule set for our app**, whose profitability still needs testing.\n\n**What the research actually supports**\n\nResearch from the New York Fed found that currency stop-loss and take-profit orders can cluster around certain levels. Crucially, triggering stops can **accelerate an existing trend**. Therefore, â€œprice crossed yesterdayâ€™s high, so sellâ€ is unreliable reasoning: the move might continue. These historical FX findings do not directly validate an SMC strategy on Exness gold. [Order clustering research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr125.html), [stop-loss cascades](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\nPractitioner sources describe a common reversal sequence: **sweep a level â†’ reclaim it â†’ break structure in the opposite direction â†’ enter using a defined confirmation or retracement rule**. This is a trading hypothesis, not proof of a profitable edge. [FXOpenâ€™s explanation](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/)\n\nCandles cannot establish which institution traded, whether hidden orders remain, or whether someone deliberately manipulated price. We should label these as **inferred liquidity levels and candidate reaction zones**. [Order-block definitions and limitations](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When would we consider a Buy?**\n\nOne coherent SMC approach is to look for the end of a small downward pullback within a broader upward trend.\n\n| Check | What we need to observe | Decision before it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Broader direction** | The higher timeframe has confirmed higher highs and higher lows. | Wait if direction is unclear. |\n| **2. Pullback location** | Price returns to a predefined area of interest, rather than entering after a large upward move. | Wait for price to arrive. |\n| **3. Downside sweep and reclaim** | Price trades below an already identified local low, then closes back above it. | A break below alone means Wait. |\n| **4. Bullish structure shift** | A later completed candle closes above a relevant, previously known local swing high. | A wick through that high is insufficient under a close-based rule. |\n| **5. Entry condition** | The selected entry model completes: confirmation entry **or** a specified retracement/rejection entry. | Do not switch models halfway through. |\n| **6. Stop and target** | A defensible stop and a target identified before entry leave sufficient potential reward after costs. | Wait or reject if they do not. |\n\nThe higher timeframe can remain bullish while the lower-timeframe pullback is bearish. A bullish CHoCH on the lower timeframe can therefore signal the **possible end of that pullback**, without implying a brand-new higher-timeframe uptrend. BOS and CHoCH must always be tied to the structure being measured. [Structure concepts](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\n**When would we consider a Sell?**\n\nUse the exact opposite sequence:\n\n1. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish.\n2. Price rallies into a predefined area of interest.\n3. It moves above an already identified local high and closes back below.\n4. A later completed candle closes below the relevant local swing low.\n5. The chosen entry condition completes.\n6. A stop above the invalidation area and a predefined downside target leave sufficient potential reward after costs.\n\n**A bullish market does not become a Sell simply because price looks expensive.** Likewise, a bearish market does not become a Buy simply because price has fallen substantially.\n\n**Continuation and reversal need separate rules**\n\nTwo common SMC approaches should be evaluated separately:\n\n| Model | Typical sequence |\n|---|---|\n| **Trend continuation** | Established trend â†’ BOS with that trend â†’ retracement into the breaking moveâ€™s candidate order block â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n| **Sweep and structure shift** | Cross a known high/low â†’ reclaim it â†’ opposite structure break â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n\nA sweep-and-shift setup can occur inside a higher-timeframe pullback or against the broader trend. Those are different experiments; we should record them separately.\n\nThere are also two different entry methods:\n\n- **Confirmation entry:** enter after the required reaction or structure break completes. It enters later and may leave less reward relative to the stop.\n- **Retracement entry:** wait for price to revisit a predetermined level or zone. It may offer a better entry price, but can remain unfilled or enter while the zone fails.\n\nNeither method is universally superior. They require separate tests using the same original setup definitions. [Entry-method trade-offs](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When should the app say Wait?**\n\nWait should be an explicit, explainable result.\n\n| Situation | App explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Structure is mixed or ranging | **WAIT â€” no directional context** |\n| Price only touches an order block/FVG | **WAIT â€” entry trigger incomplete** |\n| Price crosses a high/low but does not reclaim it | **WAIT â€” sweep/rejection unconfirmed** |\n| Reclaim occurs without the required structure break | **WAIT â€” structure confirmation missing** |\n| The model requires a retracement and none occurs | **WAIT â€” awaiting retracement** |\n| Price has already moved close to the target | **REJECT â€” insufficient remaining reward** |\n| The zone or setupâ€™s invalidation boundary is breached | **CANCEL â€” setup invalidated** |\n| Confirmation takes too long | **CANCEL â€” setup expired** |\n| Spread, quotes, market permissions, or data fail checks | **BLOCK â€” execution conditions unsuitable** |\n| Position or exposure limits are reached | **BLOCK â€” risk limit reached** |\n\nA news exclusion window can also be tested, but its timing must be defined in advance rather than added after observing losing trades.\n\n**My proposed first experiment for MarketLink**\n\nTo start with manageable rules, I would test the following **demo research model**. These settings are design choices, not proven optimal values.\n\n| Element | Initial experimental definition |\n|---|---|\n| Context timeframe | H1 |\n| Trigger timeframe | M5 |\n| Swing detection | A pivot with two completed candles on each side |\n| Bullish/bearish context | Latest two confirmed highs **and** lows both rising/falling; otherwise Wait |\n| Sweep | Cross a local level known before the candle began, then close back inside |\n| Confirmation | A subsequent candle closes beyond the opposite pre-sweep swing |\n| Confirmation expiry | Six M5 candles |\n| Entry baseline | First tradable quote after confirmation completes |\n| Stop | Beyond the sweep extreme, with a predefined buffer |\n| Target | A relevant opposing swing selected and frozen before entry |\n| Reward/risk gate | Test a minimum **2:1 after estimated costs** |\n| Position handling | One position; fixed initial SL/TP; one attempt per setup |\n\nFor this baseline, order-block and FVG retracement entries would be **separate later variants**. That lets us measure whether each addition improves results.\n\nSwing timing matters: a two-right-candle pivot becomes known only after those later candles close. A historical label placed on the pivot candle must not allow the backtest to enter there earlier. [MetaQuotesâ€™ five-bar fractal definition](https://www.mql5.com/en/code/30)\n\n**Why entry price can change Buy into Wait**\n\nConsider hypothetical prices, before costs:\n\n- Entry **100**, stop **98**, target **105**: potential reward/risk is **5 Ã· 2 = 2.5:1**.\n- If entry is delayed until **104**, with the same stop and target: **1 Ã· 6 â‰ˆ 0.17:1**.\n\nThe direction might still be bullish, but the second trade fails the proposed entry gate. We should not move the target farther away merely to manufacture an attractive ratio.\n\nA 2:1 target also does not guarantee profitability; the realized win rate, losses, slippage, and costs determine expectancy.\n\n**What matters specifically for your MT5 bridge**\n\nExness documents that a Buy opens at **Ask** and closes at **Bid**, while a Sell opens at **Bid** and closes at **Ask**. Backtests must use those executable prices, spread, commission, and slippage. A chart-only touch is insufficient to assume a fill. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\nThe app should eventually display the **current decision, completed conditions, missing condition, invalidation level, target, and signal time**. We should avoid displaying an invented â€œ90% confidenceâ€ score.\n\nBefore enabling strategy-driven orders, freeze the rules, evaluate them chronologically on unseen data, record every attempted variant, and forward-test on demo. Repeatedly choosing the best-looking backtest can produce a convincing strategy with no durable edge. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[Retained project conventions for broker-specific data and untouched evaluation]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "**Buy when bullish context, a completed bullish trigger, and acceptable trade economics agree. Sell when the bearish equivalents agree. Otherwise, Wait.**\n\nThe research supports turning SMC into a **sequence of conditions**. An order block, FVG, sweep, or CHoCH by itself does not establish an entry.\n\nSMC also has no single standardized strategy. Below I distinguish common trading practice from **a proposed rule set for our app**, whose profitability still needs testing.\n\n**What the research actually supports**\n\nResearch from the New York Fed found that currency stop-loss and take-profit orders can cluster around certain levels. Crucially, triggering stops can **accelerate an existing trend**. Therefore, â€œprice crossed yesterdayâ€™s high, so sellâ€ is unreliable reasoning: the move might continue. These historical FX findings do not directly validate an SMC strategy on Exness gold. [Order clustering research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr125.html), [stop-loss cascades](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\nPractitioner sources describe a common reversal sequence: **sweep a level â†’ reclaim it â†’ break structure in the opposite direction â†’ enter using a defined confirmation or retracement rule**. This is a trading hypothesis, not proof of a profitable edge. [FXOpenâ€™s explanation](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/)\n\nCandles cannot establish which institution traded, whether hidden orders remain, or whether someone deliberately manipulated price. We should label these as **inferred liquidity levels and candidate reaction zones**. [Order-block definitions and limitations](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When would we consider a Buy?**\n\nOne coherent SMC approach is to look for the end of a small downward pullback within a broader upward trend.\n\n| Check | What we need to observe | Decision before it happens |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Broader direction** | The higher timeframe has confirmed higher highs and higher lows. | Wait if direction is unclear. |\n| **2. Pullback location** | Price returns to a predefined area of interest, rather than entering after a large upward move. | Wait for price to arrive. |\n| **3. Downside sweep and reclaim** | Price trades below an already identified local low, then closes back above it. | A break below alone means Wait. |\n| **4. Bullish structure shift** | A later completed candle closes above a relevant, previously known local swing high. | A wick through that high is insufficient under a close-based rule. |\n| **5. Entry condition** | The selected entry model completes: confirmation entry **or** a specified retracement/rejection entry. | Do not switch models halfway through. |\n| **6. Stop and target** | A defensible stop and a target identified before entry leave sufficient potential reward after costs. | Wait or reject if they do not. |\n\nThe higher timeframe can remain bullish while the lower-timeframe pullback is bearish. A bullish CHoCH on the lower timeframe can therefore signal the **possible end of that pullback**, without implying a brand-new higher-timeframe uptrend. BOS and CHoCH must always be tied to the structure being measured. [Structure concepts](https://www.luxalgo.com/library/indicator/smart-money-concepts-smc/)\n\n**When would we consider a Sell?**\n\nUse the exact opposite sequence:\n\n1. Higher-timeframe structure is bearish.\n2. Price rallies into a predefined area of interest.\n3. It moves above an already identified local high and closes back below.\n4. A later completed candle closes below the relevant local swing low.\n5. The chosen entry condition completes.\n6. A stop above the invalidation area and a predefined downside target leave sufficient potential reward after costs.\n\n**A bullish market does not become a Sell simply because price looks expensive.** Likewise, a bearish market does not become a Buy simply because price has fallen substantially.\n\n**Continuation and reversal need separate rules**\n\nTwo common SMC approaches should be evaluated separately:\n\n| Model | Typical sequence |\n|---|---|\n| **Trend continuation** | Established trend â†’ BOS with that trend â†’ retracement into the breaking moveâ€™s candidate order block â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n| **Sweep and structure shift** | Cross a known high/low â†’ reclaim it â†’ opposite structure break â†’ selected entry trigger. |\n\nA sweep-and-shift setup can occur inside a higher-timeframe pullback or against the broader trend. Those are different experiments; we should record them separately.\n\nThere are also two different entry methods:\n\n- **Confirmation entry:** enter after the required reaction or structure break completes. It enters later and may leave less reward relative to the stop.\n- **Retracement entry:** wait for price to revisit a predetermined level or zone. It may offer a better entry price, but can remain unfilled or enter while the zone fails.\n\nNeither method is universally superior. They require separate tests using the same original setup definitions. [Entry-method trade-offs](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**When should the app say Wait?**\n\nWait should be an explicit, explainable result.\n\n| Situation | App explanation |\n|---|---|\n| Structure is mixed or ranging | **WAIT â€” no directional context** |\n| Price only touches an order block/FVG | **WAIT â€” entry trigger incomplete** |\n| Price crosses a high/low but does not reclaim it | **WAIT â€” sweep/rejection unconfirmed** |\n| Reclaim occurs without the required structure break | **WAIT â€” structure confirmation missing** |\n| The model requires a retracement and none occurs | **WAIT â€” awaiting retracement** |\n| Price has already moved close to the target | **REJECT â€” insufficient remaining reward** |\n| The zone or setupâ€™s invalidation boundary is breached | **CANCEL â€” setup invalidated** |\n| Confirmation takes too long | **CANCEL â€” setup expired** |\n| Spread, quotes, market permissions, or data fail checks | **BLOCK â€” execution conditions unsuitable** |\n| Position or exposure limits are reached | **BLOCK â€” risk limit reached** |\n\nA news exclusion window can also be tested, but its timing must be defined in advance rather than added after observing losing trades.\n\n**My proposed first experiment for MarketLink**\n\nTo start with manageable rules, I would test the following **demo research model**. These settings are design choices, not proven optimal values.\n\n| Element | Initial experimental definition |\n|---|---|\n| Context timeframe | H1 |\n| Trigger timeframe | M5 |\n| Swing detection | A pivot with two completed candles on each side |\n| Bullish/bearish context | Latest two confirmed highs **and** lows both rising/falling; otherwise Wait |\n| Sweep | Cross a local level known before the candle began, then close back inside |\n| Confirmation | A subsequent candle closes beyond the opposite pre-sweep swing |\n| Confirmation expiry | Six M5 candles |\n| Entry baseline | First tradable quote after confirmation completes |\n| Stop | Beyond the sweep extreme, with a predefined buffer |\n| Target | A relevant opposing swing selected and frozen before entry |\n| Reward/risk gate | Test a minimum **2:1 after estimated costs** |\n| Position handling | One position; fixed initial SL/TP; one attempt per setup |\n\nFor this baseline, order-block and FVG retracement entries would be **separate later variants**. That lets us measure whether each addition improves results.\n\nSwing timing matters: a two-right-candle pivot becomes known only after those later candles close. A historical label placed on the pivot candle must not allow the backtest to enter there earlier. [MetaQuotesâ€™ five-bar fractal definition](https://www.mql5.com/en/code/30)\n\n**Why entry price can change Buy into Wait**\n\nConsider hypothetical prices, before costs:\n\n- Entry **100**, stop **98**, target **105**: potential reward/risk is **5 Ã· 2 = 2.5:1**.\n- If entry is delayed until **104**, with the same stop and target: **1 Ã· 6 â‰ˆ 0.17:1**.\n\nThe direction might still be bullish, but the second trade fails the proposed entry gate. We should not move the target farther away merely to manufacture an attractive ratio.\n\nA 2:1 target also does not guarantee profitability; the realized win rate, losses, slippage, and costs determine expectancy.\n\n**What matters specifically for your MT5 bridge**\n\nExness documents that a Buy opens at **Ask** and closes at **Bid**, while a Sell opens at **Bid** and closes at **Ask**. Backtests must use those executable prices, spread, commission, and slippage. A chart-only touch is insufficient to assume a fill. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\nThe app should eventually display the **current decision, completed conditions, missing condition, invalidation level, target, and signal time**. We should avoid displaying an invented â€œ90% confidenceâ€ score.\n\nBefore enabling strategy-driven orders, freeze the rules, evaluate them chronologically on unseen data, record every attempted variant, and forward-test on demo. Repeatedly choosing the best-looking backtest can produce a convincing strategy with no durable edge. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:236-237|note=[Retained project conventions for broker-specific data and untouched evaluation]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 7, "text_sha256": "88df92c7f682633b211a56ce4642d840448ce170fcdbe6a2b4ac30ddc07274a1", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 177, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T09:02:00.244Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad09670556c8191a6a5d277c7176bd2", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**Yesâ€”changing the variables can improve a strategyâ€™s results. But it can also produce â€œextreme profitâ€ on historical data that disappears in live trading.** The task is to find settings that continue working on unseen data, after realistic costs.\n\nFor our SMC model, these are the main adjustable variables. **The example values below are research choices, not proven profitable settings.**\n\n| Variable | What it controls | Example values or choices |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Context timeframe** | Which chart defines the broader direction | M15, H1, H4 |\n| **Entry timeframe** | Which chart supplies the trigger | M1, M3, M5, M15 |\n| **Swing sensitivity** | How many candles on each side confirm a high/low | 2, 3, or 5 per side |\n| **Sweep distance** | How far price must cross a known level | A fixed number of points or a fraction of volatility |\n| **Reclaim rule** | What confirms price returned inside the level | Same-candle close or a later close |\n| **Structure-break rule** | What qualifies as BOS/CHoCH | Wick crossing versus completed candle close |\n| **Displacement threshold** | How strong the confirmation candle must be | Body â‰¥ 0.6, 0.8, or 1.0 Ã— ATR |\n| **Setup expiry** | How long confirmation remains eligible | 3, 6, or 12 candles after the sweep |\n| **Entry method** | When to enter after confirmation | Next available quote, order-block retest, or FVG retest |\n| **Zone definition** | How an order block or FVG is constructed | Full candle range, candle body, minimum gap size |\n| **Stop buffer** | Extra distance beyond the invalidation level | 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n| **Target rule** | Where to take profit | A structural swing or a fixed reward multiple |\n| **Trade filters** | Conditions that permit or block an entry | Session, spread, volatility, news window |\n| **Exit management** | What happens after entry | Fixed SL/TP, trailing stop, break-even, time exit |\n\n**ATR measures recent price volatility.** Any ATR-based condition should use information available before the decision. Definitions, confirmation delays, entries, and exits must remain consistent throughout a test. [Order-block research guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**Some variables affect exposure rather than the quality of the signal.**\n\nLot size, leverage, and risk per trade can dramatically increase historical profit **and losses**. Increasing them does not make the entry prediction more accurate. We should compare strategy settings using consistent position sizing.\n\nSpread, commission, and slippage are execution assumptions. We must model them realistically. Exness buys execute at Ask and sells at Bid; using one candle price for everything can distort results. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\n**For the first experiment, I would change only four variables.**\n\nKeep H1 context, M5 triggers, completed-candle rules, ATR period 14, entry method, target rule, and position sizing fixed.\n\n| Variable | Three experimental values |\n|---|---|\n| Swing confirmation length | 2 / 3 / 5 candles per side |\n| Confirmation body Ã· prior completed M5 ATR | 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 |\n| Confirmation expiry after sweep | 3 / 6 / 12 M5 candles |\n| Stop buffer beyond sweep extreme | 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n\nThat gives **3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 = 81 combinations**. We should also compare the displacement filter against a baseline without it.\n\n**How we decide whether an improvement is credible:**\n\n1. Develop settings on the earliest data.\n2. Compare candidates on later validation data.\n3. Freeze the selected rules before evaluating an untouched final period.\n4. Record every attempted configuration, including failures.\n5. Check nearby settings: a broad region of acceptable results is more reassuring than one exceptional combination.\n6. Forward-test the frozen version on demo.\n\nRepeatedly searching the same history increases the chance of selecting noise. Even a holdout becomes compromised if we keep using its results to redesign the strategy. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\nWe should measure **net expectancy, profit factor, drawdown, trade count, exposure, and stability across periods**. No parameter combination can be called profitable for our app yetâ€”we have verified the trading bridge, but have not backtested this SMC strategy.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:237-237|note=[Preserved untouched evaluation data and complete candidate logging]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "**Yesâ€”changing the variables can improve a strategyâ€™s results. But it can also produce â€œextreme profitâ€ on historical data that disappears in live trading.** The task is to find settings that continue working on unseen data, after realistic costs.\n\nFor our SMC model, these are the main adjustable variables. **The example values below are research choices, not proven profitable settings.**\n\n| Variable | What it controls | Example values or choices |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Context timeframe** | Which chart defines the broader direction | M15, H1, H4 |\n| **Entry timeframe** | Which chart supplies the trigger | M1, M3, M5, M15 |\n| **Swing sensitivity** | How many candles on each side confirm a high/low | 2, 3, or 5 per side |\n| **Sweep distance** | How far price must cross a known level | A fixed number of points or a fraction of volatility |\n| **Reclaim rule** | What confirms price returned inside the level | Same-candle close or a later close |\n| **Structure-break rule** | What qualifies as BOS/CHoCH | Wick crossing versus completed candle close |\n| **Displacement threshold** | How strong the confirmation candle must be | Body â‰¥ 0.6, 0.8, or 1.0 Ã— ATR |\n| **Setup expiry** | How long confirmation remains eligible | 3, 6, or 12 candles after the sweep |\n| **Entry method** | When to enter after confirmation | Next available quote, order-block retest, or FVG retest |\n| **Zone definition** | How an order block or FVG is constructed | Full candle range, candle body, minimum gap size |\n| **Stop buffer** | Extra distance beyond the invalidation level | 0.1, 0.2, or 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n| **Target rule** | Where to take profit | A structural swing or a fixed reward multiple |\n| **Trade filters** | Conditions that permit or block an entry | Session, spread, volatility, news window |\n| **Exit management** | What happens after entry | Fixed SL/TP, trailing stop, break-even, time exit |\n\n**ATR measures recent price volatility.** Any ATR-based condition should use information available before the decision. Definitions, confirmation delays, entries, and exits must remain consistent throughout a test. [Order-block research guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/)\n\n**Some variables affect exposure rather than the quality of the signal.**\n\nLot size, leverage, and risk per trade can dramatically increase historical profit **and losses**. Increasing them does not make the entry prediction more accurate. We should compare strategy settings using consistent position sizing.\n\nSpread, commission, and slippage are execution assumptions. We must model them realistically. Exness buys execute at Ask and sells at Bid; using one candle price for everything can distort results. [Exness execution rules](https://get.exness.help/hc/en-us/articles/360017885880-Available-order-and-execution-types)\n\n**For the first experiment, I would change only four variables.**\n\nKeep H1 context, M5 triggers, completed-candle rules, ATR period 14, entry method, target rule, and position sizing fixed.\n\n| Variable | Three experimental values |\n|---|---|\n| Swing confirmation length | 2 / 3 / 5 candles per side |\n| Confirmation body Ã· prior completed M5 ATR | 0.6 / 0.8 / 1.0 |\n| Confirmation expiry after sweep | 3 / 6 / 12 M5 candles |\n| Stop buffer beyond sweep extreme | 0.1 / 0.2 / 0.3 Ã— ATR |\n\nThat gives **3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 Ã— 3 = 81 combinations**. We should also compare the displacement filter against a baseline without it.\n\n**How we decide whether an improvement is credible:**\n\n1. Develop settings on the earliest data.\n2. Compare candidates on later validation data.\n3. Freeze the selected rules before evaluating an untouched final period.\n4. Record every attempted configuration, including failures.\n5. Check nearby settings: a broad region of acceptable results is more reassuring than one exceptional combination.\n6. Forward-test the frozen version on demo.\n\nRepeatedly searching the same history increases the chance of selecting noise. Even a holdout becomes compromised if we keep using its results to redesign the strategy. [Research on backtest overfitting](https://www.davidhbailey.com/dhbpapers/backtest-prob.pdf)\n\nWe should measure **net expectancy, profit factor, drawdown, trade count, exposure, and stability across periods**. No parameter combination can be called profitable for our app yetâ€”we have verified the trading bridge, but have not backtested this SMC strategy.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:237-237|note=[Preserved untouched evaluation data and complete candidate logging]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 8, "text_sha256": "8d5fc3589a6f785f7277d99f6e3866fc3798674ede15886e2d4f6b9d625b2df2", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
{"file": "rollout-2026-10-15T13-43-10-01a13e83-da25-79c2-8413-bcf2766efa8a.jsonl", "line": 313, "timestamp": "2026-10-15T09:11:49.953Z", "message_id": "msg_0c38f0064b42ade4016ad0989b79c08191af15872d189090ea", "role": "assistant", "phase": "final_answer", "parts": ["**My recommendation for our app is to start with an SMC continuation model: higher-timeframe direction â†’ confirmed structure break â†’ first order-block retracement â†’ a defined entry confirmation.**\n\nThat is a practical starting point because its decisions can be recorded and tested clearly. **The research does not establish a universally most profitable SMC strategy.** Different educators teach different entry and management rules, and public teaching material rarely proves which rules generated someoneâ€™s advertised returns.\n\nI compared original material from **MentFX, JeaFX, Photon Trading, LuxAlgo, FXOpen and ATAS**, examined an open-source implementation, and checked SMC-specific research.\n\n**What the educators actually teach**\n\n| Source | Published approach | Entry and management details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MentFX** | Establish market context, then follow an **impulse â†’ correction â†’ impulse** sequence. | Its workbookâ€™s long template uses a buy-stop at the patternâ€™s high, SL below the correction low, **3R target**, and break-even at **1R**. It explicitly presents this as one possible approach. [Original workbook](https://mentfx.com/workbook.pdf) |\n| **JeaFX** | Analyse from higher to lower timeframes, identify areas of interest, then confirm through structure. | Its example puts SL below the entry zone, targets an opposing supply area, moves to break-even at **2R**, and uses structural trailing and partial exits. These are examples, not universal settings. [Original lesson](https://jeafx.com/lesson/building-trading-systems-2/) |\n| **Photon Trading** | Use **structure for direction, supply/demand for timing, and liquidity to refine entries**. | Its public guide discusses zone reactions, candlestick confirmation, stops at invalidation, and partial exits at meaningful levels. The public pages do not fully specify its proprietary system numerically. [Framework](https://www.photontradingfx.com/trade-recap-summary), [SMC guide](https://www.photontradingfx.com/blog/how-do-smart-money-concepts-work) |\n| **LuxAlgo** | Map internal/swing structure, then investigate order-block retracements and relevant targets. | Its detailed guidance distinguishes limit entries from confirmation entries and requires explicit zone construction, confirmation time, invalidation, costs and exits. [Original guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/) |\n\nI could verify these **published teaching methods**. The material examined did not let me verify comparable, independently audited, long-term performance attributable to each exact strategy.\n\nOne useful disagreement is already visible: **break-even at 1R, break-even at 2R, and structural trailing are different systems**. We should test them separately.\n\n**The strongest common lesson is the order of decisions**\n\nAcross these approaches, the useful workflow is:\n\n1. **Context:** Which direction, if any, does the relevant structure support?\n2. **Location:** At what predefined price area would a trade become interesting?\n3. **Trigger:** What completed event permits an entry?\n4. **Invalidation:** What price action proves this particular setup has failed?\n5. **Exit:** Where will profit be taken, and how will the position be managed?\n6. **Execution:** Does the actual entry still make sense after spread, fees and slippage?\n\nThis sequence is more useful for our app than collecting additional SMC terminology.\n\nMy interpretation is that several â€œconfluencesâ€ can describe the same price move. A displacement candle, structure break and nearby FVG should not automatically count as three independent reasons to expect success.\n\n**The main SMC strategies worth separating**\n\n| Model | Typical Buy sequence | What we must test |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Structure-break continuation** | Bullish context â†’ bullish BOS â†’ retracement to the breaking moveâ€™s order block â†’ selected trigger. | Whether the retracement and trigger improve on a simple structure-break baseline. |\n| **Sweep-and-reversal** | Cross a known low â†’ reclaim it â†’ bullish structure shift â†’ retracement/confirmation entry. | Whether the reclaim and shift distinguish reversals from continued selling. |\n| **Breaker retest** | A bearish order block fails upward â†’ structure supports the change â†’ its retest becomes a possible Buy location. | Exact failure, retest and invalidation definitions. |\n| **Sweep continuation â€” research control** | Price crosses a known high and continues holding above it â†’ continuation entry under fixed rules. | Whether continuation performs better than the commonly taught reversal interpretation. |\n\nSell rules mirror the directional conditions.\n\nFXOpen describes the sweep â†’ CHoCH â†’ retracement approach, with stops beyond the sweep extreme. Its breaker explanation describes failed zones becoming possible references after a structure change. These are practitioner models to investigate. [Liquidity-grab approach](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/), [order blocks and breakers](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/order-blocks-and-breaker-blocks-of-the-smart-money-concept/)\n\nThe fourth model deserves inclusion because **crossing a liquidity reference does not necessarily mean a reversal**. Bank-order research found that triggered stops can reinforce trends and produce rapid price cascades. [New York Fed research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\n**The first rule set I recommend testing**\n\nFor our first SMC-only experiment, I would use the following specification. **This is our proposed adaptation of the research, not a claim to reproduce an educatorâ€™s complete system.**\n\nFor a **Buy**:\n\n1. **Confirm bullish context.** Use completed higher-timeframe information. Define precisely which highs and lows establish the trend.\n2. **Require an upward structure break.** A completed execution-timeframe candle must close above an eligible swing high that was already known.\n3. **Create the candidate order block.** Select the last qualifying bearish candle before the breaking move, using one fixed search rule and fixed boundaries.\n4. **Wait for its first subsequent retest.** The block becomes eligible only after the break confirms it.\n5. **Require a defined reaction.** For example, a retest candle enters the zone and closes back above its upper boundary.\n6. **Enter using the next available executable quote.** Do not assume the entry occurred earlier at the best price inside the candle.\n7. **Place SL beyond the predefined invalidation boundary**, with a stated buffer.\n8. **Choose the target before entry.** Use an eligible opposing higher-timeframe level or a fixed-R target as separate test variants.\n9. **Reject the entry if insufficient reward remains.** A minimum net 2:1 reward/risk is a reasonable experimental gate; it is not a universal SMC law.\n\nFor a **Sell**, reverse the direction: bearish context, downward close through structure, bullish origin candle, retest, bearish rejection, stop above invalidation and a predefined downside target.\n\nH1 context and M5 execution can remain our initial experimental timeframes. The educatorsâ€™ material does **not** establish that H1/M5 is the best combination.\n\nInitially, I would keep position management simple with fixed SL/TP. Then compare:\n\n- Fixed exits.\n- Break-even at 1R.\n- Break-even at 2R.\n- Structural trailing.\n- Partial exits.\n\nThat lets us discover whether an entry has useful expectancy before allowing management changes to obscure the result.\n\n**When the model should refuse a trade**\n\nThe app should give a specific reason when it says Wait or Cancel:\n\n- **No clear context:** structure is mixed.\n- **No confirmed break:** price only wicked through a level.\n- **No eligible retest:** the selected entry sequence remains incomplete.\n- **Invalidated zone:** price crossed the predefined failure boundary.\n- **Expired setup:** the permitted waiting period ended.\n- **Late entry:** the available target is now too close.\n- **Execution blocked:** quotes, spread, broker constraints or exposure limits fail checks.\n\nA valid setup can still lose. We must preserve those losses rather than retrospectively relabelling every failed setup as â€œinducementâ€ or a â€œfake order block.â€\n\n**What the performance research changes**\n\nThere are now studies specifically examining SMC, so it would be inaccurate to say no research exists. Their findings are mixed.\n\n- **A 2026 journal paper on XAUUSD H1** reports positive results from a hybrid system using SMC features, calibrated machine-learning probabilities and constrained sizing. Its headline **46.7% return is from the development period**, January 2023â€“May 2025. The later holdout emphasizes calibration and downside diagnostics. Hourly OHLC and proxy costs also limit transfer to our Exness tick execution. It supports further investigation, not a conclusion that plain SMC is best. [Published paper](https://www.informatica.si/index.php/informatica/article/download/12899/6712)\n- **A recent G10 FX working paper** reports weaknesses in textbook reversal interpretations and some promising continuation/fade alternatives. Its strongest variants still fail a deflated-Sharpe check at defensible trial counts. I reviewed its indexed abstract; it is not audited live performance. [Working paper](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7430998)\n- **A daily-stock working paper** tested 44 formalized detectors and found none beat appropriately matched random entries after multiple-testing correction. That result concerns daily US equities, not every intraday gold strategy. [Study](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7483658)\n\nTogether, these findings argue for comparing competing explanations on our own data.\n\n**One implementation finding is especially important**\n\nThe open-source Python SMC library uses future candles to confirm swings and FVGs, and some outputs are written onto earlier rows. Some historical annotations can also change after later events.\n\nThose calculations can help chart analysis, but using their final historical labels directly as entry-time signals can inflate a backtest. Our engine must preserve **where a pattern originated, when it became detectable, and when it was invalidated**. [Actual implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/joshyattridge/smart-money-concepts/master/smartmoneyconcepts/smc.py)\n\nFor MarketLink, I recommend comparing **simple structure breaks, BOS-and-retest continuation, and sweep-and-shift reversal** under identical costs and position sizing. Add a filter only when it improves results on later, unseen data. The first SMC module should make every decision visible in replay, including failed setups and reasons to Wait.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:237-237|note=[Preserved complete candidate logging and untouched evaluation conventions]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>"], "text": "**My recommendation for our app is to start with an SMC continuation model: higher-timeframe direction â†’ confirmed structure break â†’ first order-block retracement â†’ a defined entry confirmation.**\n\nThat is a practical starting point because its decisions can be recorded and tested clearly. **The research does not establish a universally most profitable SMC strategy.** Different educators teach different entry and management rules, and public teaching material rarely proves which rules generated someoneâ€™s advertised returns.\n\nI compared original material from **MentFX, JeaFX, Photon Trading, LuxAlgo, FXOpen and ATAS**, examined an open-source implementation, and checked SMC-specific research.\n\n**What the educators actually teach**\n\n| Source | Published approach | Entry and management details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **MentFX** | Establish market context, then follow an **impulse â†’ correction â†’ impulse** sequence. | Its workbookâ€™s long template uses a buy-stop at the patternâ€™s high, SL below the correction low, **3R target**, and break-even at **1R**. It explicitly presents this as one possible approach. [Original workbook](https://mentfx.com/workbook.pdf) |\n| **JeaFX** | Analyse from higher to lower timeframes, identify areas of interest, then confirm through structure. | Its example puts SL below the entry zone, targets an opposing supply area, moves to break-even at **2R**, and uses structural trailing and partial exits. These are examples, not universal settings. [Original lesson](https://jeafx.com/lesson/building-trading-systems-2/) |\n| **Photon Trading** | Use **structure for direction, supply/demand for timing, and liquidity to refine entries**. | Its public guide discusses zone reactions, candlestick confirmation, stops at invalidation, and partial exits at meaningful levels. The public pages do not fully specify its proprietary system numerically. [Framework](https://www.photontradingfx.com/trade-recap-summary), [SMC guide](https://www.photontradingfx.com/blog/how-do-smart-money-concepts-work) |\n| **LuxAlgo** | Map internal/swing structure, then investigate order-block retracements and relevant targets. | Its detailed guidance distinguishes limit entries from confirmation entries and requires explicit zone construction, confirmation time, invalidation, costs and exits. [Original guidance](https://www.luxalgo.com/blog/how-to-find-order-blocks-obs-in-trading/) |\n\nI could verify these **published teaching methods**. The material examined did not let me verify comparable, independently audited, long-term performance attributable to each exact strategy.\n\nOne useful disagreement is already visible: **break-even at 1R, break-even at 2R, and structural trailing are different systems**. We should test them separately.\n\n**The strongest common lesson is the order of decisions**\n\nAcross these approaches, the useful workflow is:\n\n1. **Context:** Which direction, if any, does the relevant structure support?\n2. **Location:** At what predefined price area would a trade become interesting?\n3. **Trigger:** What completed event permits an entry?\n4. **Invalidation:** What price action proves this particular setup has failed?\n5. **Exit:** Where will profit be taken, and how will the position be managed?\n6. **Execution:** Does the actual entry still make sense after spread, fees and slippage?\n\nThis sequence is more useful for our app than collecting additional SMC terminology.\n\nMy interpretation is that several â€œconfluencesâ€ can describe the same price move. A displacement candle, structure break and nearby FVG should not automatically count as three independent reasons to expect success.\n\n**The main SMC strategies worth separating**\n\n| Model | Typical Buy sequence | What we must test |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Structure-break continuation** | Bullish context â†’ bullish BOS â†’ retracement to the breaking moveâ€™s order block â†’ selected trigger. | Whether the retracement and trigger improve on a simple structure-break baseline. |\n| **Sweep-and-reversal** | Cross a known low â†’ reclaim it â†’ bullish structure shift â†’ retracement/confirmation entry. | Whether the reclaim and shift distinguish reversals from continued selling. |\n| **Breaker retest** | A bearish order block fails upward â†’ structure supports the change â†’ its retest becomes a possible Buy location. | Exact failure, retest and invalidation definitions. |\n| **Sweep continuation â€” research control** | Price crosses a known high and continues holding above it â†’ continuation entry under fixed rules. | Whether continuation performs better than the commonly taught reversal interpretation. |\n\nSell rules mirror the directional conditions.\n\nFXOpen describes the sweep â†’ CHoCH â†’ retracement approach, with stops beyond the sweep extreme. Its breaker explanation describes failed zones becoming possible references after a structure change. These are practitioner models to investigate. [Liquidity-grab approach](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/liquidity-grabs-identification-and-trading-strategies/), [order blocks and breakers](https://fxopen.com/blog/en/order-blocks-and-breaker-blocks-of-the-smart-money-concept/)\n\nThe fourth model deserves inclusion because **crossing a liquidity reference does not necessarily mean a reversal**. Bank-order research found that triggered stops can reinforce trends and produce rapid price cascades. [New York Fed research](https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr150.html)\n\n**The first rule set I recommend testing**\n\nFor our first SMC-only experiment, I would use the following specification. **This is our proposed adaptation of the research, not a claim to reproduce an educatorâ€™s complete system.**\n\nFor a **Buy**:\n\n1. **Confirm bullish context.** Use completed higher-timeframe information. Define precisely which highs and lows establish the trend.\n2. **Require an upward structure break.** A completed execution-timeframe candle must close above an eligible swing high that was already known.\n3. **Create the candidate order block.** Select the last qualifying bearish candle before the breaking move, using one fixed search rule and fixed boundaries.\n4. **Wait for its first subsequent retest.** The block becomes eligible only after the break confirms it.\n5. **Require a defined reaction.** For example, a retest candle enters the zone and closes back above its upper boundary.\n6. **Enter using the next available executable quote.** Do not assume the entry occurred earlier at the best price inside the candle.\n7. **Place SL beyond the predefined invalidation boundary**, with a stated buffer.\n8. **Choose the target before entry.** Use an eligible opposing higher-timeframe level or a fixed-R target as separate test variants.\n9. **Reject the entry if insufficient reward remains.** A minimum net 2:1 reward/risk is a reasonable experimental gate; it is not a universal SMC law.\n\nFor a **Sell**, reverse the direction: bearish context, downward close through structure, bullish origin candle, retest, bearish rejection, stop above invalidation and a predefined downside target.\n\nH1 context and M5 execution can remain our initial experimental timeframes. The educatorsâ€™ material does **not** establish that H1/M5 is the best combination.\n\nInitially, I would keep position management simple with fixed SL/TP. Then compare:\n\n- Fixed exits.\n- Break-even at 1R.\n- Break-even at 2R.\n- Structural trailing.\n- Partial exits.\n\nThat lets us discover whether an entry has useful expectancy before allowing management changes to obscure the result.\n\n**When the model should refuse a trade**\n\nThe app should give a specific reason when it says Wait or Cancel:\n\n- **No clear context:** structure is mixed.\n- **No confirmed break:** price only wicked through a level.\n- **No eligible retest:** the selected entry sequence remains incomplete.\n- **Invalidated zone:** price crossed the predefined failure boundary.\n- **Expired setup:** the permitted waiting period ended.\n- **Late entry:** the available target is now too close.\n- **Execution blocked:** quotes, spread, broker constraints or exposure limits fail checks.\n\nA valid setup can still lose. We must preserve those losses rather than retrospectively relabelling every failed setup as â€œinducementâ€ or a â€œfake order block.â€\n\n**What the performance research changes**\n\nThere are now studies specifically examining SMC, so it would be inaccurate to say no research exists. Their findings are mixed.\n\n- **A 2026 journal paper on XAUUSD H1** reports positive results from a hybrid system using SMC features, calibrated machine-learning probabilities and constrained sizing. Its headline **46.7% return is from the development period**, January 2023â€“May 2025. The later holdout emphasizes calibration and downside diagnostics. Hourly OHLC and proxy costs also limit transfer to our Exness tick execution. It supports further investigation, not a conclusion that plain SMC is best. [Published paper](https://www.informatica.si/index.php/informatica/article/download/12899/6712)\n- **A recent G10 FX working paper** reports weaknesses in textbook reversal interpretations and some promising continuation/fade alternatives. Its strongest variants still fail a deflated-Sharpe check at defensible trial counts. I reviewed its indexed abstract; it is not audited live performance. [Working paper](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7430998)\n- **A daily-stock working paper** tested 44 formalized detectors and found none beat appropriately matched random entries after multiple-testing correction. That result concerns daily US equities, not every intraday gold strategy. [Study](https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7483658)\n\nTogether, these findings argue for comparing competing explanations on our own data.\n\n**One implementation finding is especially important**\n\nThe open-source Python SMC library uses future candles to confirm swings and FVGs, and some outputs are written onto earlier rows. Some historical annotations can also change after later events.\n\nThose calculations can help chart analysis, but using their final historical labels directly as entry-time signals can inflate a backtest. Our engine must preserve **where a pattern originated, when it became detectable, and when it was invalidated**. [Actual implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/joshyattridge/smart-money-concepts/master/smartmoneyconcepts/smc.py)\n\nFor MarketLink, I recommend comparing **simple structure breaks, BOS-and-retest continuation, and sweep-and-shift reversal** under identical costs and position sizing. Add a filter only when it improves results on later, unseen data. The first SMC module should make every decision visible in replay, including failed setups and reasons to Wait.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:237-237|note=[Preserved complete candidate logging and untouched evaluation conventions]\n</citation_entries>\n<rollout_ids>\n01a0ded5-02c2-7e50-af95-787a44881dff\n</rollout_ids>\n</oai-mem-citation>", "images": [], "source_kind": "original_assistant_output", "index": 9, "text_sha256": "03e0be0700f7ef64a96eb92ca3f35a9e08b190cbb85dc2391e216d8fd617fa94", "section": "original_answer_variant"}
